{"offset": 100, "next": 200, "data": [{"citingPaper": {"paperId": "adf24071fc463072f790918451c1cdac498b1fa8", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33956", "CorpusId": 294367022}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/adf24071fc463072f790918451c1cdac498b1fa8", "title": "Continual Learning Exam: Benchmarking Long-Horizon RSI and Experiential Learning in LLM Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33956, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2508880707", "name": "Hao-Yu Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2306396908", "name": "Si-Tian Tu"}, {"authorId": "2348490567", "name": "Yuxuan Huang"}, {"authorId": "2375284697", "name": "Hui-Chi Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2155273086", "name": "Zhangyue Yin"}, {"authorId": "2520542265", "name": "Jia-Hao Huang"}, {"authorId": "14745697", "name": "Jing-Yuan Xu"}, {"authorId": "2520023722", "name": "Tian-Miao Shi"}, {"authorId": "2510696762", "name": "Jin-Song Shi"}, {"authorId": "2522084696", "name": "Nian Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2479579929", "name": "Yangzhihao Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2520141130", "name": "Zhuo-Lang Chan"}, {"authorId": "2520106324", "name": "Hui-Yan Hu"}, {"authorId": "2446444133", "name": "Chen-Chao Sheng"}, {"authorId": "2112601905", "name": "Ya-Qing Luo"}, {"authorId": "2487644709", "name": "Yue Chen"}, {"authorId": "2520118688", "name": "Hua-Xiu Yao"}, {"authorId": "2325905652", "name": "Meng Fang"}, {"authorId": "2257572528", "name": "Jun Wang"}], "abstract": "Continual learning is the ability of LLM agents to improve over time by acquiring, refining, and applying knowledge from experience while retaining useful prior knowledge. It is essential for agents learning over long time horizons, yet current evaluations largely assess individual task performance rather than improvement through accumulated experience. We introduce Continual Learning Exam (CLE), a large-scale benchmark developed by 50+ domain experts to measure experience-driven improvement in realistic professional workflows. CLE comprises 120 human-authored tasks, each organised as a five-level ladder, yielding 600 levels spanning 89 professional roles across 12 occupational domains. Agents must pass each level to unlock the next, with later levels building on experience accumulated during earlier assignments. An evaluation agent assesses each submission against human-authored rubrics. We compare a single-submission setting with one that provides diagnostic feedback after failure and allows one revision. Across 17 harness-model configurations using frontier LLMs, feedback and revision yield a median completion gain of 5.3 percentage points. Yet the best-performing system clears only 47.7% of levels and completes all five levels in just 23.3% of tasks, highlighting the difficulty of sustained learning from experience for existing frontier LLMs. As such, the dataset can also be used to post-train LLMs to strengthen their continual learning capabilities."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "730b21e7e7a3210498573684e58412ff58bbc2d3", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.34238", "CorpusId": 294364774}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/730b21e7e7a3210498573684e58412ff58bbc2d3", "title": "$\\Delta\\tau$-Bench: Pushing Agents Toward a Moving Capability", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34238, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2190751119", "name": "Shuo-Fei Qiao"}, {"authorId": "2387896316", "name": "Xuan-Guang Pan"}, {"authorId": "2340722387", "name": "Yijun Chen"}, {"authorId": "2386094453", "name": "Wen-Di Feng"}, {"authorId": "2187129580", "name": "Bin Wu"}, {"authorId": "2278840339", "name": "Run-Nan Fang"}, {"authorId": "2283182145", "name": "Yu-Qi Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2253472695", "name": "Shu-Min Deng"}, {"authorId": "2371990732", "name": "Emine Yilmaz"}], "abstract": "In this paper, we introduce $\\Delta\\tau$-Bench, an executable agent benchmark that models the structural evolution of large tool ecosystems. In $\\Delta\\tau$-Bench, independently evolving services continually reshape how tasks can be accomplished: familiar tools may disappear or be replaced, while more efficient alternatives emerge amid overlapping functionality and noisy documentation. Against this moving capability frontier, we evaluate whether agents can proactively discover efficient tool-use paths and promptly revise their accumulated procedural knowledge, bringing together demands that existing benchmarks fail to examine. $\\Delta\\tau$-Bench comprises 1,588 validated multi-turn tasks across 42 domains and six successive ecosystem states generated through capability-preserving transformations. Our evaluation shows that even the strongest evaluated model or harness achieves only 42.3% task success, while initial gains from existing skill-learning methods can not consistently translate into better adaptation as the ecosystem evolves. These findings motivate Steward, an ecosystem manager that jointly manages tools and procedural knowledge through incremental skill maintenance and dynamic provisioning during task execution. Experiments show that Steward sustains higher task success across ecosystem changes with lower skill-maintenance overhead, while improving both recovery from disrupted workflows and adoption of more efficient alternatives."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6234b3968ed05cb495a37eae16d133daf9effc84", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.34043", "CorpusId": 294366264}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6234b3968ed05cb495a37eae16d133daf9effc84", "title": "Holding a Steady Course: Knowledge Evolution from Expertise and Lessons for Continual Agent Learning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34043, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2284912807", "name": "Wenzhe Fan"}, {"authorId": "2108813860", "name": "Benjamin Steenhoek"}, {"authorId": "2267452746", "name": "Gaurav Mittal"}, {"authorId": "2112337704", "name": "Yu Hu"}], "abstract": "Autonomous agents are increasingly deployed across sequences of related tasks, yet they often fail to retain and improve their knowledge over time. Existing approaches convert interaction trajectories into memories or reusable skills, but experience is not automatically reliable knowledge. Automatically collected skills and memories can propagate errors, mislead, and corrupt useful knowledge. To address this gap, we introduce Knowledge Evolution from Expertise and Lessons (KEEL), an agent-agnostic framework that uses verifier evidence to transform successful behavior into reusable skills and failures into corrective lessons. As new evidence accumulates, individual skill claims can be supported, contradicted, replaced, or retired. On SkillFlow benchmark, KEEL improves task success by 4.2 to 21.7 percentage points over the no-knowledge baseline, compared with gains of 2.4 to 9.6 points from SkillFlow's default method. Ablations show that skills and lessons are complementary. These results show that reliable continual agent learning depends not simply on accumulating experience, but on continually validating and evolving the knowledge derived from it. Our code is available at: https://github.com/microsoft/KEEL-TTCL26."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c113b04a15c1d4057d4b8f2777dd3f4b9e3a4f26", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.34450", "CorpusId": 294367222}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c113b04a15c1d4057d4b8f2777dd3f4b9e3a4f26", "title": "EvolveMAS: A Unified Self-Evolution Framework for Decentralized Multi-Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34450, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2364789332", "name": "Ya-Lun Wu"}, {"authorId": "2357498174", "name": "Qi-Wei Ma"}, {"authorId": "2372232199", "name": "Bing-Bing Xu"}, {"authorId": "2266277013", "name": "Dan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2075398318", "name": "Long Bai"}, {"authorId": "2261851935", "name": "Bo-Yang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2259858493", "name": "Jia-Tong Li"}, {"authorId": "2382965039", "name": "Zhe-Qi Lv"}, {"authorId": "1429199889", "name": "Shao-Zu Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2118951553", "name": "Pei-Ying Wang"}, {"authorId": "2506267525", "name": "Jia-Wei Wang"}, {"authorId": "2311074323", "name": "An Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2351809900", "name": "Tat-Seng Chua"}], "abstract": "Decentralized multi-agent systems (MAS) enable large language model agents to interact without centralized control. Agents need to adapt to other agents at test time and reuse accumulated experience to refine their interaction strategies for future interactions across cooperative, mixed-motive, and adversarial settings. However, existing approaches often develop adaptation mechanisms for particular interaction settings. This raises a central question: how can decentralized MAS support test-time adaptation and reliable experience reuse across interaction settings? We propose EvolveMAS, a unified self-evolution framework that supports experience-driven adaptation within and across episodes. EvolveMAS pairs each main agent with a dedicated MemoryAgent for test-time memory governance, which maintains isolated Structured Memory outside the main agent's reasoning context. EvolveMAS organizes adaptation into two complementary processes: Within-Episode Online Adaptation and Cross-Episode Knowledge Evolution. During Within-Episode Online Adaptation, MemoryAgent updates episodic, semantic, and working memory from interaction evidence to inform subsequent actions. During Cross-Episode Knowledge Evolution, the main agent uses memory and episode outcomes to distill reusable interaction knowledge comprising partner profiles and interaction strategies for subsequent episodes. Across five benchmarks and six evaluation configurations spanning the three interaction settings, EvolveMAS achieves the highest normalized average score of 77.2 among the compared methods. Ablations show that test-time memory governance and cross-episode knowledge reuse both improve performance. Interaction traces further illustrate how updated memory and distilled knowledge guide subsequent interactions. These results support experience-driven adaptation in decentralized MAS without updating model parameters."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "886a1998b96df61ee7459d584f766a54482d179d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.34500", "CorpusId": 294366645}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/886a1998b96df61ee7459d584f766a54482d179d", "title": "CaliMem: Search-Efficient Data Agent via Calibrated Memory", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34500, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "1949334927", "name": "Han-Ze Li"}, {"authorId": "2329081678", "name": "Athan Z. Li"}, {"authorId": "2054842299", "name": "Fei-Yang Kang"}, {"authorId": "2130518057", "name": "Jia-Chen T. Wang"}, {"authorId": "2475498001", "name": "Yu-Han Chen"}, {"authorId": "2357923277", "name": "Rui-Shan Liu"}, {"authorId": "2268321162", "name": "Ruo-Xi Jia"}], "abstract": "Data curation is a first-order design choice in machine learning, yet tailoring training data to a downstream model and task remains costly in labor, expertise, and compute. Datasets are therefore often processed through fixed pipelines and reused across applications. We show that this assumption is fragile: across 55 dataset--model tasks spanning biological domains, the same curation recipe can help one model while hurting another. Effective curation must adapt to the downstream setting, motivating automated search over data recipes. We first build an agentic system that searches for curation policies at deployment, improving data utility via \\textit{test-time curation (TTC)}. Although achieving performance gains, its search is driven largely by recent feedback and remains local. Adding persistent memory helps the agent retain evidence across iterations and reveals a deeper problem: it forms claims that extend beyond the conditions in which they were observed, sometimes abandoning an entire strategy after a single unsuccessful trial. To address this problem, we introduce CaliMem, a calibrated-memory agentic loop that requires the agent to predict the outcome of each proposed policy before executing it. Comparing this prediction with the observed result gives the agent a \\textit{reality check} on the claims behind its proposal. It uses the prediction error to retain supported claims, revise mistaken ones, and specify the conditions under which a conclusion holds. This makes the agent's beliefs testable, reduces overgeneralization, and supports more effective long-horizon exploration. Across biological and LLM post-training tasks, CaliMem consistently outperforms fixed curation baselines and published methods, and exceeds full-data training by up to 19.4\\%. Code is open-sourced."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "62712cd8621cb7ed0a4fb0370c91f8316dd28544", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.03685", "CorpusId": 295507384}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/62712cd8621cb7ed0a4fb0370c91f8316dd28544", "title": "LOLPlay: Benchmarking Real-Time Visual Agents in League of Legends", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03685, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2302532793", "name": "Zhao-Jian Yu"}], "abstract": "Visual agents need to coordinate reasoning and action while their environment evolves during inference. We introduce LOLPlay, a benchmark in native \\emph{League of Legends} with 36 champion-specific tasks spanning resource management, combat, cooperation, and objectives. Agents interact through screenshots and mouse--keyboard actions; automated initialization and 82 executable subgoal checks distinguish partial progress from completion. Across 324 task evaluations of three models at three reasoning-effort settings, GPT-6 Astra achieves the highest task success rate of 25.0\\% at low and medium effort, and the highest subgoal success rate of 40.3\\% at medium effort. However, high effort improves neither metric over low effort for any model. For Astra, longer requests leave fewer interaction rounds, suggesting a cost to additional reasoning in real-time control. Trajectories reveal incomplete subgoal composition and actions executing after the observed scene changes, motivating agents that combine visual reasoning with timely action."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "bc1306df633b8067cea2f628202d8bbf58af2fdf", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33487", "CorpusId": 294227692}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bc1306df633b8067cea2f628202d8bbf58af2fdf", "title": "ReSight: Task-Driven Self-Improving Memory for Embodied Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33487, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-24", "authors": [{"authorId": "2423412076", "name": "Li-Rong Che"}, {"authorId": "2473776641", "name": "Yang Chen"}, {"authorId": "2486727700", "name": "Yi-Meng Su"}, {"authorId": "34776599", "name": "Da-Qi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2354550156", "name": "Zhen-Yu Huang"}, {"authorId": "2507855939", "name": "Ji-Aan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2316673843", "name": "Yuzhe Yang"}, {"authorId": "2467100130", "name": "Xu Cao"}, {"authorId": "2345700914", "name": "Chuang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2430939627", "name": "Jiang-Feng Su"}, {"authorId": "2329880419", "name": "Xue-Qian Wang"}], "abstract": "Robots can use historical observations to answer spatiotemporal queries in familiar environments, but the presence of relevant evidence does not imply that existing tools support the operations a task requires. We introduce ReSight, a task-driven self-improving memory system for embodied agents that expands the capabilities for processing and accessing historical observations as task demands arise. ReSight retains keyframes and their spatiotemporal associations, separating observation evidence from extensible derived fields so that stored observations can be reprocessed for new needs. An execution agent and a coding agent share this memory through different interfaces: the former calls registered tools within a bounded online budget, while the latter uses a programmable workspace and a larger exploration budget to inspect evidence, write programs, and acquire models as needed. To realize capability expansion in ReSight, we develop Explore-Distill-Mount (EDM), which identifies capability gaps from execution and exploration records without task reference answers and converts exploration-derived solutions into reusable memory builders, query tools, and skills, extending memory representations and access capabilities as needed. Experiments on three public benchmarks demonstrate effective base memory access and capability expansion. With the execution model and online budget held fixed within each benchmark, EDM reduces mean position error on OC-NaVQA from 33.10 m to 24.72 m and improves high-level success on FindingDory from 38.3% to 63.8%. With the tools and skill frozen, success still improves by 28.3 percentage points on FindingDory episodes excluded from exploration and distillation. Experiments on real robot patrol memories further demonstrate reuse of these capabilities for subsequent queries and new memories, with fewer online tool calls on average."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "de0794fbfc9197c0fcde9b9a304e5fb8637ec6af", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33690", "CorpusId": 294227947}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/de0794fbfc9197c0fcde9b9a304e5fb8637ec6af", "title": "PACE: Research Proving Agent for Adaptive Conjecture Exploration", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33690, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-24", "authors": [{"authorId": "2484755943", "name": "Shu-Rong Miao"}, {"authorId": "2505395222", "name": "Fei-Ming Wang"}, {"authorId": "2346447046", "name": "Wen-Tao Long"}, {"authorId": "2292085498", "name": "Chen-Yi Li"}, {"authorId": "2365041040", "name": "Kun Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2242221741", "name": "Zai-Wen Wen"}], "abstract": "Many mathematical research agents focus on solving problems with fixed statements. However, conjecture research, especially in applied mathematics, may require revising candidate questions or conclusions as investigation proceeds, coupling problem formulation with proof development. We present PACE, a research-proof agent that supports this process through a structured research loop that selects mathematical objectives and develops or challenges candidate mechanisms. The whole prover constructs proofs from these mechanisms, and an independent verifier checks them against the original target. Evidence-tracking memory stores assumptions and dependencies arising during research and marks evidence for revalidation when a candidate or condition changes. This memory supports task-specific context composition that keeps checked premises, exploratory material and unresolved obligations distinct. To evaluate proof construction and conjecture research, we introduce ResearchProofBench, an applied-mathematics benchmark focused on optimization, comprising 51 known-proof tasks with 331 hidden checkpoints under historical abstract access and six open-problem cases. The known-proof tasks assess reconstruction of published results, while the open-problem cases assess mathematical investigation and proof development. On the 51 known-proof tasks, PACE achieves an aggregate Proof Score of 75.16 and completes 20 proofs. In conjecture research, PACE develops conditional results, with its submissions receiving higher review scores than the compared baselines."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c76b65d2f5acbd8e97aa15b1b859f0a7dd84ed55", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33396", "CorpusId": 294228091}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c76b65d2f5acbd8e97aa15b1b859f0a7dd84ed55", "title": "Fast Instinct, Slow Reason: A Dual-Speed Agentic Framework for Self-Configuring Multi-Object Tracking in Satellite Video", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33396, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-24", "authors": [{"authorId": "2403017878", "name": "Fan He"}, {"authorId": "2433738369", "name": "Bai-Ning Wu"}, {"authorId": "2362525915", "name": "Yan Li"}, {"authorId": "2272681609", "name": "Xiao-Yang Zeng"}], "abstract": "Multi-object tracking in satellite video underpins persistent monitoring, yet offline-tuned trackers lack the onboard intelligence and efficiency to adapt within an observation of tens of seconds. The root is that adaptation still means relabelling and retraining the detector, which cannot run on board in time. Tuning the tracker configuration strongly controls accuracy without touching the network, yet search needs labelled or proxy evaluations, and a language agent costs seconds and tokens per decision. We propose a dual-speed agentic framework pairing a fast instinct compiled offline with a slow reason serving the mission. Its algorithm, Arbitration of Unlabelled Shortlists from Prior Experience, or AUSPEX, learns a covering shortlist and a label-free ranker from labelled source tasks and commits one configuration per sequence. Its system, a Cascaded Onboard Runtime with Verified Uplinked Skills, or CORVUS, cascades reflex, skill, and agent layers, so that a verified skill commits 2.5 s into each observation while a language agent serves natural- language missions. On SatMTB and SatVideoDT with three recent detectors, AUSPEX raises IDF1 by 2.84 to 2.94 points and MOTA by 3.21 to 3.29 points on average without target labels, balances accuracy and labels best among sixteen methods, and loses at most 0.28 IDF1 points at 1.25 tracking passes. CORVUS executes four missions with execution accuracy up to 0.99 and target recall at the perception ceiling, spends no token on the routine decision, runs on Jetson Xavier NX, and hosts fifteen language models from six families without altering any commitment, demonstrating its extensibility and generalization."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6797ae04494ec47ca10899546c8bdd8fab5f5af1", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33861", "CorpusId": 294365962}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6797ae04494ec47ca10899546c8bdd8fab5f5af1", "title": "ReGE: Stronger Agents through Experience Synthesis", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33861, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-24", "authors": [{"authorId": "2388706461", "name": "Jia-Rong Liang"}, {"authorId": "2238120100", "name": "Yubo Wang"}, {"authorId": "2342746625", "name": "Zi-Jie Liu"}, {"authorId": "2191206519", "name": "Yuxuan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2343748885", "name": "Ping Nie"}, {"authorId": "2343777493", "name": "Wen-Hu Chen"}], "abstract": "Repeated attempts at interactive tasks uncover facts, useful operations, and failures that can inform subsequent execution. We introduce ReGE, a framework for turning this recorded experience into guidance for a fresh attempt. Its main implementation, ReGE-Syn, uses a single coach call to jointly synthesize bounded execution records and available artifacts into self-contained guidance, connecting task-specific findings with proposed actions and relevant pitfalls. A student starts from the task's initial environment state and adapts this guidance to current observations. Source generation, synthesis, and student execution use the same base model, without parameter updates or a stronger teacher. Across four benchmarks, ReGE improves accuracy by an average of 9.9 percentage points for GPT and 8.8 percentage points for Claude compared with unguided execution. On matched BrowseComp-Plus subsets, ReGE-Syn reduces guidance-construction tokens by 98.2\\% on GPT and 95.5\\% on Claude relative to adapted AggAgent, with observed success. Combined construction and execution tokens decrease by 15.7\\% and 35.4\\%, respectively, counting input plus output and excluding source generation. BrowseComp-Plus ablations further associate access to process information with fewer student steps and show that joint synthesis produces substantially shorter guidance than Direct history or Independent summaries, with observed success. These results demonstrate how agents can use their own recorded experience to improve subsequent task completion, and suggest a low-cost way to consolidate the execution records of parallel agent systems such as agent swarms."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1374308cd138cb2f4bb0ff4e8739974da0b4f28d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.32026", "CorpusId": 293935942}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1374308cd138cb2f4bb0ff4e8739974da0b4f28d", "title": "RSI-Opt: Recursive Self-Improvement Optimizer Agents For Model Training", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32026, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-23", "authors": [{"authorId": "2195352476", "name": "Bing-Hang Lu"}, {"authorId": "2308106471", "name": "Yi-Ge Sun"}, {"authorId": "2364827101", "name": "Guang Lin"}], "abstract": "Conventional neural-network training typically follows a predefined optimization recipe, motivating a LLM agent that monitors training dynamics and adjusts optimizer settings as training progresses, but a fixed agent policy does not accumulate experience across runs. We introduce RSI-Opt, a plug-and-play Recursive Self-Improvement Optimizer Agents which evolves the knowledge that guides this process: a prompt, a playbook of procedures, a library of tested optimizer code, and a decision cadence. A meta-agent rewrites this policy using evidence from earlier runs, and an archive retains evaluated policies for further evolution. Across 5 generations on three training tasks, the meta-agents develop reusable procedures and code and conduct their own CPU proxy experiments. RSI-Opt substantially improves optimizer-agent performance, with representative raw-space summary $z$-scores increasing from $+10.48$ to $+14.33$ on the evolution suite with Opus 5.5 and from $-0.36$ to $+3.11$ on held-out tasks with GPT-5.5 through cross-model policy transfer. On the Burgers PDE, it reduces mean relative $L_2$ error by approximately 2.7-fold with Opus 5.5 and 8.8-fold with GPT-6.1 over the fixed-prompt agent baseline, although PDE transfer varies across LLMs and tasks. Controlled comparisons show that the evolved policy combines reusable optimization knowledge with adaptation to each task: initial decisions capture most observed gains on seen tasks, while continued adaptation improves outcomes on unseen tasks. These findings demonstrate how procedures and executable tools accumulated across training runs can improve optimizer-agent performance and support transfer to scientific machine learning."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4154b7163019a95290d72af2f1ffef2610e76c1c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.32620", "CorpusId": 293940411}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4154b7163019a95290d72af2f1ffef2610e76c1c", "title": "What Transfers Across Protein Pockets? Experience Reuse in Molecular Optimization for Agentic Structure-Based Drug Design", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32620, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science", "Biology"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-23", "authors": [{"authorId": "2325083980", "name": "Zai-Fei Yang"}, {"authorId": "2313733355", "name": "Wei-Yu Chen"}, {"authorId": "2388121151", "name": "Ya-Qing Wang"}, {"authorId": "2313730122", "name": "James T. Kwok"}], "abstract": "LLM agents optimize a ligand in a protein pocket by editing it step by step and docking each edit. Recent agents first spend extra dockings probing the complex with controlled edits; guidance induced from these probes is reported to help more than the chemical knowledge the model already carries. The probes are then discarded with the instance that produced them, so the obvious next step is to keep them in an experience base, letting a new pocket inherit what earlier ones already paid for. Such a base is read through a \\emph{key}: the record properties under which past edits are pooled and a new one is looked up. Using the full log of one such run over the CrossDocked2020 test-set pockets, we ask whether its keys recur on a pocket the base has never seen and still separate improving from worsening edits. \\crchange{Using} the contacts at the site being edited, the action, and the round as \\crchange{a} key, the base separates them sharply. Within our analysis, this separation is largely explained by how much the edit changed the molecule's size, the action, and the round, none of which describes the target. That is not because the target is irrelevant: naming it outright separates them several times more sharply. For a pocket absent from the base, transfer must rely on a description rather than its identity. Among the exact-match descriptions we evaluate, the more specific ones rarely recur, whereas the coarser ones provide little separation; the signals that do transfer primarily describe the edit and the molecule it produced rather than the target. Under the representations and lookup scheme studied here, an experience base may be more useful for prioritizing an agent's own proposals for docking than for directly prescribing what to build."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "9f8cae94445770c36e6a3e59c10f9b17e15ffbf9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.32348", "CorpusId": 293940264}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9f8cae94445770c36e6a3e59c10f9b17e15ffbf9", "title": "LogLook: Execution-Grounded Memory for Frontier-Model Robot Planners", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32348, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-23", "authors": [{"authorId": "2410305588", "name": "Tian-Yi Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2137388105", "name": "Tian-Yao Tong"}, {"authorId": "13974169", "name": "Zhen-Shan Bing"}], "abstract": "Many manipulation tasks require a robot to remember what it has done and what it has seen. Frontier vision-language models can plan such tasks without task-specific training, but how such a planner should remember an episode is an open question. We argue that its memory must keep track of what the planner has done and learned, and must let the planner choose what to look at, since every frame costs tokens; a note rewritten at every step meets both poorly. We propose LogLook, whose execution-grounded memory lets the system keep the record, an execution log and the model's earlier notes, while the model decides where to look: frames are returned only where it expects a change, and demonstrations are skimmed before it inspects the parts it chooses. Measured with the benchmark's motion planner, LogLook matches the success of a note-only memory with 43% fewer tokens and less than half the images, and fails far fewer episodes through what it remembered or saw. With direct control, LogLook solves 93.5% of the RoboMME test set."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4f2714079432936d86f0192f67bd7b2965d3dc6a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.32813", "CorpusId": 293940253}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4f2714079432936d86f0192f67bd7b2965d3dc6a", "title": "HarnessMM: Recursive Self-Improvement for Harness Token Efficiency", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32813, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-23", "authors": [{"authorId": "2345305718", "name": "Yu-Hua Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2509359032", "name": "Chang-Feng Xu"}, {"authorId": "2390234953", "name": "Fei-Fei Gao"}, {"authorId": "2299119265", "name": "Li Zhao"}], "abstract": "HarnessMinusMinus (HarnessMM) is a research project on improving the efficiency of agent harnesses, the software coordinating model reasoning, tool execution, and feedback. This technical report consolidates its shared framework, implementations, and empirical studies across coding, tool-based, and visual-agent settings. Agent harnesses often invoke reasoning after each tool result, even when earlier calls have already determined subsequent actions. HarnessMM addresses this overhead through guarded decision reuse and recursive self-improvement (RSI). RSI refines author prompts, context settings, and adapter rules using training feedback, with validation selecting deployment configurations. At inference time, a controller reuses resolved decisions while keeping actor weights fixed. Executable Decision Graphs encode actions, arguments, dependencies, and guards. Guarded Decision Reuse activates eligible decisions from observed feedback, while Certified Callback Substitution executes them through native tool interfaces, replacing redundant reasoning callbacks. The original actor resumes when new reasoning is required or reuse conditions are unmet. On HumanEval, HarnessMM preserves Codex success at 90.82\\% while reducing tokens per task from 66.72k to 47.02k (29.54\\% saving). For DeepSeek Harness, success improves from 71.43\\% to 85.71\\% while tokens decrease from 96.28k to 11.62k (87.93\\% saving). An MBPP ablation shows 26.91\\% lower cost than single-use graphs and 19.52\\% lower cost than retained callbacks. The report also summarizes the HarnessMM-V visual-agent study and documents optimization overhead and execution time where measured. Together, these studies investigate guarded decision reuse across diverse agent harness settings. Code is available at https://github.com/YuhuaJiang2002/HarnessMM."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d818e659eda8fd8857b5fa6537fa1a5b2c54f833", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.63808/tcs.v2i2.519", "CorpusId": 294328652}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d818e659eda8fd8857b5fa6537fa1a5b2c54f833", "title": "Autonomous Agents Based on Large Language Models: Reasoning Strategies and Multi-Agent Interaction", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://system.wisacad-pub.com/index.php/tcs/article/download/519/406", "status": "HYBRID", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.63808/tcs.v2i2.519?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.63808/tcs.v2i2.519, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-10-23", "authors": [{"authorId": "2458994816", "name": "Zheng-Yuan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2280730786", "name": "Yu-Yao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2284455447", "name": "Yi-Xin Deng"}], "abstract": "Language-model agents acquire capability along two routes that published work almost always varies together, one enlarging the computation a single agent performs and the other enlarging the number of agents and the channels along which they pass partial results, which leaves the contribution of each route indistinguishable. This paper separates an agent system into a reasoning operator and an interaction topology, holding the number of agents fixed at three across all multi-agent topologies, together with the number of rounds, the message cap, the prompt skeleton, and the memory settings for all combinations, and evaluates a full factorial design of four reasoning levels and four topologies on three public test sets totalling 484 instances under five random seeds, giving 38,720 task executions. Comparisons are paired at the instance level, and effects are estimated from logistic mixed-effects models fitted to each test set separately. The reasoning factor spans 18.0 points on the static suite whereas the topology factor spans 7.8 points on the interactive one, a reversal indicating that the two mechanisms act on different failure modes. The topology span is taken across all four topology levels and therefore includes the step from one agent to three, against a structure-only span of 2.8 points among the multi-agent topologies. The two factors also depart from additivity in a localised manner. A mechanism-motivated single-degree-of-freedom contrast on the only cell in which both factors contribute a corrective pass is significant at p = 0.001 on the static suite, whereas the omnibus nine-degree-of-freedom interaction test is not (p = 0.094), and in that cell the observed 50.0 percent lies 5.8 points below the additive expectation. After tokens are counted, seven of the sixteen configurations remain undominated on the static suite and five on the operating-system suite, and returns saturate at 12,400 tokens per instance on the static suite under the present design."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1cd8660abed8c40e827defd897c1613a1e651685", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.1038/s43586-026-00534-y", "CorpusId": 293864279}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1cd8660abed8c40e827defd897c1613a1e651685", "title": "Agentic AI systems for scientific discovery", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://infoscience.epfl.ch/handle/20.500.14299/268478", "status": "GREEN", "license": "CCBYNCND", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1038/s43586-026-00534-y?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1038/s43586-026-00534-y, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-10-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "2516658250", "name": "Bin Feng"}, {"authorId": "2184609760", "name": "Peter Walther"}, {"authorId": "153823942", "name": "Lei-Li Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2338378638", "name": "Reid Coyle"}, {"authorId": "2271932587", "name": "Seongmin Kim"}, {"authorId": "2304182964", "name": "Abhijoy Mandal"}, {"authorId": "2316409920", "name": "Zi-Jian Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2238421088", "name": "Sven M. Papidocha"}, {"authorId": "2516655735", "name": "Anna Kelmanson"}, {"authorId": "2400548676", "name": "David Ming Segura"}, {"authorId": "2203851640", "name": "Saumya Thakur"}, {"authorId": "94982353", "name": "Shyam Chand Pal"}, {"authorId": "8800375", "name": "Varinia Bernales"}, {"authorId": "2153575781", "name": "Wen-Hao Gao"}, {"authorId": "2346109637", "name": "Xin Yang"}, {"authorId": "2162547030", "name": "Le Cong"}, {"authorId": "1379965853", "name": "P. Schwaller"}, {"authorId": "2250025906", "name": "Al\u00e1n Aspuru-Guzik"}, {"authorId": "2376488118", "name": "Zhi-Ling Zheng"}], "abstract": "Artificial intelligence (AI) agents are emerging in scientific research as systems that can transform the accumulated decisions of scientists into traceable workflows. In fact, modern scientific practice depends on continual coordination of knowledge dispersed across literature, data, theory and experiment, which, however, is often made through manual, case-by-case judgement. This Primer introduces agentic AI systems as a general method to structure this process through natural language interfaces, serving as a computational brain in the laboratory. Central to this framework are practical workflows for in-depth literature search and knowledge mining, orchestration of physics-based simulations, and integration with automated experimental platforms. We begin by describing the foundations of AI agents, including task decomposition, memory, tool use and communication between agents, and by distinguishing single-agent and multi-agent designs, as well as fully autonomous and human-in-the-loop configurations. Equally important are the means by which these systems are evaluated, documented and reported reproducibly. Finally, we examine major bottlenecks, including hallucination, tool misuse, limited interpretability and safety risks, and outline a future in which coordinated networks of agents, instruments and researchers transform scientific discovery from isolated tasks into continuous adaptive experimentation. Agentic artificial intelligence (AI) systems enhance scientific workflows by structuring knowledge coordination through natural language interfaces. In this Primer, Feng et al. examine the challenges and opportunities involved in integrating AI agents in the laboratory."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "fa36b2ae708193d205c89153acf820fbbacac101", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.30790", "CorpusId": 293894514}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fa36b2ae708193d205c89153acf820fbbacac101", "title": "Agentic Knowledge-Model Co-Evolution via Consistency Regularization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30790, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "2211606453", "name": "Sirui Chen"}, {"authorId": "2257083296", "name": "Zhi-Ning Liu"}, {"authorId": "2362854122", "name": "Chao Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2303885774", "name": "Jing-Rui He"}, {"authorId": "2273766311", "name": "Zheng Li"}], "abstract": "LLM agents can evolve by learning from accumulated experience. Existing approaches extract reusable external knowledge, such as skills and memories, from experience to guide subsequent task solving, with some further internalizing this knowledge through model training. However, different ways of analyzing and transforming the same experience can yield knowledge with substantially different utility, even when the resulting guidance appears reasonable. Inspired by consistency regularization, we identify consistent utility gains across different ways of analyzing the same experience, rather than relying on any single way of analyzing it. We introduce SPLICE, a framework for consistency-regularized knowledge-model co-evolution. It constructs evolution views by perturbing how the same experience is analyzed, producing different knowledge candidates. Knowledge updates are then guided by common gain, which accounts for utility gains and their consistency across views. During model evolution, self-distillation internalizes the evolved knowledge alongside reinforcement learning, using knowledge associated with consistent utility degradation as a contrastive reference. The two processes interleave, with model updates driving further knowledge evolution. Experiments across diverse domains show that SPLICE outperforms knowledge evolution and model training baselines by up to 7.1 and 4.6 points in average accuracy, while its evolved knowledge reduces output tokens per correct solution by up to 44% and transfers across model sizes and families."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "aca7140a861cbee738109f30cb2bb9626eb22188", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.31162", "CorpusId": 293894465}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/aca7140a861cbee738109f30cb2bb9626eb22188", "title": "HiSTEVE 1.0: Harnessing Human-Interface VLA Agents for Open-World Games", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31162, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "2402857712", "name": "Ze-Cong Tang"}, {"authorId": "2455604779", "name": "Zi-Feng Guan"}, {"authorId": "2391832765", "name": "Yun-Cheng Guo"}, {"authorId": "2316516702", "name": "Zhan-Qiu Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2318303849", "name": "Yi-Wen Guo"}, {"authorId": "2333237487", "name": "Bo Dai"}], "abstract": "Building virtual embodied agents that operate in open-ended games like human players is a long-standing goal requiring both long-horizon autonomy and reliable native-interface control. As a procedurally generated open world, Minecraft provides a natural testbed for studying the integration of these capabilities. Existing agents typically provide either native visuomotor control without long-horizon autonomy or high-level planning executed through environment APIs or predefined skills. To bridge this gap, we propose HiSTEVE, a hierarchical VLA agent with a capability-aware agent harness. At the orchestration level, we equip the harness with structured memory for tracking progress and a VLM planner for selecting subtasks within the VLA's capabilities. To reliably determine subtask completion, we further introduce a policy-coupled task completion state detector that conditions on VLA policy context to signal candidate completion. At the control level, a single VLA executes all subtasks with vision-only perception and native keyboard-and-mouse actions. To mitigate interference between heterogeneous GUI and non-GUI interactions, we introduce regime-specific RMSNorm branches and separate mouse-movement token embeddings. We also train the VLA through world knowledge injection, imitation learning, and reinforcement learning with environment feedback to improve reliability and recovery. HiSTEVE achieves state-of-the-art atomic-task performance, scoring 85.0% on MineStudio Bench and a 37.4% average success rate on OpenHA, while also completing long-horizon tasks such as crafting an iron pickaxe from scratch like human players."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f3cde83293e92dbeb40df7b28da50ff3a45b6640", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.31034", "CorpusId": 293894206}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3cde83293e92dbeb40df7b28da50ff3a45b6640", "title": "CANNBot-CAKE: Collaborative Agentic Kernel Evolution for Ascend NPU", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31034, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "2452537473", "name": "Deng Pan"}, {"authorId": "2516983810", "name": "Xue-Jian Gao"}, {"authorId": "2290318708", "name": "Jun-Yi Fan"}, {"authorId": "2452538545", "name": "Bin Du"}, {"authorId": "2479282931", "name": "Jia-Sheng Li"}, {"authorId": "2454210612", "name": "Xin-Xian Chen"}, {"authorId": "2516888290", "name": "Nok-Yan Chang"}, {"authorId": "2504354370", "name": "Xinlin Li"}, {"authorId": "2383066909", "name": "Ling-Chao Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2383854073", "name": "Jun Li"}, {"authorId": "2383326945", "name": "Yu-Wei Fan"}], "abstract": "Efficient custom operators are essential for modern deep learning workloads, yet developing high-performance kernels for Ascend NPUs remains slow, expert-intensive, and difficult to automate. Although AI agents now approach expert performance on GPU kernel benchmarks, current models remain less capable of generating AscendC kernels for NPUs, potentially reflecting the relative scarcity of relevant training data. We thus present CAKE, a CANN-native system for deployable custom operators that combines adaptive multi-DSL routing, separate Generation and Evolution modes, and reviewed knowledge reuse through CAKE Book and a Self-Evolving Agent. On 53 CANN-Bench v1.1 operators on Ascend 950PR, CAKE's aggregated results achieve $100\\%$ case pass rate on the standard set (1,060/1,060) and $98.28\\%$ on the hidden set (4,167/4,240), with an overall mean score of 78.63 out of 100 and an average performance speedup of $1.334\\times$ over the baseline. Evolution then pushes correct operators further: EngramGateFusion cuts aggregate device time by $46.6\\%$ over 20 rounds and roughly halves the mean roofline gap. These results offer a practical path toward closing Ascend's AI-for-kernel capability gap."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "bccbdf83fc6896245e048321a079b85c43c9bd4e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.31789", "CorpusId": 293936318}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bccbdf83fc6896245e048321a079b85c43c9bd4e", "title": "Phronix: The Capability-Centric Learning Paradigm for Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31789, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "46251636", "name": "Hariharan Devarajan"}, {"authorId": "2075512227", "name": "Neeraj Rajesh"}, {"authorId": "36210022", "name": "A. Marathe"}, {"authorId": "2134146", "name": "Nikoli Dryden"}, {"authorId": "2498163500", "name": "Kin-Hong Ng"}, {"authorId": "2239205012", "name": "Michela Taufer"}], "abstract": "Scientific computing increasingly relies on large language model-based agents for performance engineering tasks such as instrumentation, analysis, and workflow porting. However, agent effectiveness depends heavily on the surrounding harness, which typically relies on manually written skills and curated memory. This limits autonomous learning, forcing agents to repeatedly rediscover solutions rather than retain and reuse knowledge from prior executions. We introduce Phronix, a capability-centric learning paradigm that enables agentic harnesses to acquire, validate, and refine reusable capabilities from their own executions. Phronix operates through a predictor-validator-corrector loop: MCP tools and associated skills propose solutions, tool executions validate them, and observed outcomes refine the stored capabilities. It distills every observed interaction into a capability store and researches a failing task rather than abandoning it. First, such learned capabilities solve a recurring task on the first attempt in 93-95% of cases across every model, significantly reducing solution non-determinism and improving reliability. Second, Phronix turns an agent's own execution into 7.6-30.8 durable capabilities per task without user instruction, and creation falls below two once the needed capability exists. Third, warm-start runs consume only 0.38-0.40 million tokens per task, which is three to four times less than a single baseline attempt, while a cold-start costs no more than the baseline. On the 1000 Genome Pegasus workflow, instrumenting a production application for I/O performance analysis, Phronix passes all 11 correctness checks against six for the baseline harness, and also lifts the task solving rates of two cheaper models from 29% and 27% of SWE-bench tasks solved to 91% and 87%, respectively, matching the performance of the most expensive evaluated model."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d5eca343e925ce23d34e7522b4ba5511d985d7e6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.31846", "CorpusId": 293936392}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d5eca343e925ce23d34e7522b4ba5511d985d7e6", "title": "Don't Forget What You Just Learned: Self-Directed Repair Turns Agent Mistakes Into Lasting Lessons", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31846, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "2326838551", "name": "Yuval Kansal"}, {"authorId": "2314695668", "name": "Karen Ullrich"}, {"authorId": "2307071171", "name": "Polina Kirichenko"}, {"authorId": "2308102420", "name": "Ilia Kulikov"}, {"authorId": "152393000", "name": "N. Jha"}, {"authorId": "2249759469", "name": "Mohammad Pezeshki"}, {"authorId": "2334522785", "name": "Mark Ibrahim"}], "abstract": "Intelligent agents should learn from their mistakes so that experience leads to continuous improvement. Yet today's agents recover from a mistake only temporarily: in-context reflections are easily forgotten across sessions and compete for limited context, while full-parameter fine-tuning is expensive and can interfere with existing capabilities. We introduce Self-Direction, a mechanism for agents to learn from mistakes that combines the benefits of temporary in-context retries and policy updates. When an agent fails a task, it reflects on its own trajectory in order to extract a lesson and immediately distills it into a lightweight low-rank adapter. By keeping the original weights frozen, Self-Direction prevents adapter training from overwriting base-model parameters while training additional parameters equivalent to ~0.02% of the base model's parameter count for each adapter. The agent then saves these adapters in a persistent library of lessons, automatically routing them to solve related problems later. Experiments with agents based on open-weight models from the Qwen family across open-ended research (GAIA), tool-use workflows (Claw-Eval), and terminal tasks (Terminal-Bench 2.1) show that our method improves overall success rates by 8.9 to 17.0 percentage points and performs competitively with frontier systems. We find that Self-Direction reduces repeated mistakes across future encounters of a task, nearly doubling the reliability of an agent's corrections. Learned lessons also transfer: a handful of adapters generalize to dozens of new tasks without additional training. Ultimately, this self-directed repair transforms everyday mistakes into lasting lessons."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f3e6b318ae0e5a15dfbbd98b56d433bbdcfbbfa9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.30108", "CorpusId": 293856854}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3e6b318ae0e5a15dfbbd98b56d433bbdcfbbfa9", "title": "Multimodal Embodied Agents: A Unified Capability-centric Perspective from Computer-Use to Robot-Use", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30108, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2261796570", "name": "Yan-Zhe Chen"}, {"authorId": "2243392790", "name": "Ziyi Yang"}, {"authorId": "2393716355", "name": "Ji-Feng Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2325916819", "name": "Qi-Ming Huang"}, {"authorId": "2516605787", "name": "Rui-He An"}, {"authorId": "2516775375", "name": "Pei-Yao Xu"}, {"authorId": "2109771638", "name": "Heejean G. Yang"}, {"authorId": "3049742", "name": "Run-Da Liu"}, {"authorId": "2057185610", "name": "Chang Gong"}, {"authorId": "2326830144", "name": "Zhi-Jun Cao"}, {"authorId": "2237427303", "name": "Ze-Chen Bai"}, {"authorId": "2400665537", "name": "Wen-Zheng Zeng"}, {"authorId": "2276342847", "name": "Yi-Qi Lin"}, {"authorId": "2294359232", "name": "Guo-Qiang Liang"}, {"authorId": "2324345511", "name": "K. Ma"}, {"authorId": "2351409697", "name": "K. Lin"}, {"authorId": "2047358650", "name": "M. Shou"}], "abstract": "Multimodal agents (MMAs) sustain interaction through reasoning, memory, tools, and feedback, most visibly as computer-use agents, while robotic systems (RSs) couple sensing and actuation under physical dynamics. We define multimodal embodied agents (MMEAs) as goal-directed systems that couple multimodal task reasoning with physical action and revise decisions from the resulting feedback. This raises a central question: what changes when a multimodal agent moves from computer-use to robot-use? We introduce PAPAV, a unified capability-centric framework of five recurring functions: Perceive the current state, Anticipate action effects, Plan a feasible course, Act through an interface or body, and Verify the outcome. Defined by function rather than architecture, these capabilities let PAPAV identify what all three share and then compare MMEAs with MMAs and with RSs. Five physical constraints, from partial observability to unverifiable outcomes, leave fewer choices fixed in advance and less room to reverse errors. Across 64 benchmarks, explicit evaluation centers on Act; only 5 assess Anticipate and 3 assess Verify, leaving both largely hidden behind task success. These constraints also frame the open challenges we identify. PAPAV therefore provides a common basis for designing reliable physical agents and measuring progress across all five capabilities rather than task success alone."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4cbceba464de78ee1b9b80778645278f9ba7fae0", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.29357", "CorpusId": 293856820}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4cbceba464de78ee1b9b80778645278f9ba7fae0", "title": "Memory-Centric Continual Learning for AI Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29357, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2430456532", "name": "Dongfang Li"}, {"authorId": "2376081703", "name": "Zhuo-En Chen"}, {"authorId": "2308272140", "name": "Bo-Nian Jia"}, {"authorId": "2324070581", "name": "Zi-Xuan Liu"}, {"authorId": "2184510032", "name": "Zheng-Xuan Lu"}, {"authorId": "2226563538", "name": "Can Deng"}, {"authorId": "2468055766", "name": "Yu-Kun Shi"}, {"authorId": "2506990795", "name": "Yu Meng"}, {"authorId": "2511091317", "name": "Xin Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2457318233", "name": "Jia-He Huang"}, {"authorId": "2457334628", "name": "Fu-Hao Li"}, {"authorId": "2457918049", "name": "Jun-Mai Wang"}, {"authorId": "2516603577", "name": "Shun-Yao Wen"}, {"authorId": "2340832203", "name": "Jing-Jun Sun"}, {"authorId": "2180827303", "name": "Jia-Xu Tian"}, {"authorId": "2500998159", "name": "Jiang Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2510472394", "name": "Zi-Han Wang"}, {"authorId": "2490873765", "name": "Liang-You Li"}, {"authorId": "2271643854", "name": "Yi-Tong Yao"}, {"authorId": "2279903067", "name": "Shuang-Yong Song"}, {"authorId": "3407212", "name": "Bei-Lun Wang"}, {"authorId": "2296713608", "name": "Peng-Xu Wei"}, {"authorId": "2515254737", "name": "Han Xu"}, {"authorId": "2312096447", "name": "Chang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2230249425", "name": "Yong-Xiang Li"}, {"authorId": "2285172247", "name": "Bao-Tian Hu"}, {"authorId": "2344661487", "name": "Xiang-Yang Ji"}, {"authorId": "2331796452", "name": "Min Zhang"}], "abstract": "Autonomous agents powered by foundation models can reason, plan, and use tools, but their fixed weights and context limits hinder sustained learning from experience over long-term deployment. While external memory systems attempt to bridge this gap, they mostly act as passive retrieval caches, evaluated primarily by whether they can recall stored facts or past interactions. Whether memory can drive genuine continual learning remains an open question. In this paper, we introduce Memory-Centric Continual Learning (MCCL), arguing that memory should be judged by whether it improves future actions, not merely what it retrieves. MCCL has three main parts. First, a three-axis taxonomy organizes memory by where state is stored (substrates), what form it takes (assets), and how it is managed (policies). This defines a two-loop lifecycle that updates memory state in an inner loop while refining management policies in an outer loop based on task feedback. Second, an evaluation protocol measures retention under interference, adaptation to change, and task transfer to isolate genuine behavioral gains from simple context expansion. Third, a memory scaling framework models how agent performance scales with experience, storage capacity, and compute budgets across non-parametric and parametric memory. Together, these components reframe agent memory from passive lookup to continuous learning, offering a principled route toward self-improving autonomous agents."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f2afce290bb870370d8ae6a0e683def4f1924fbf", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.29326", "CorpusId": 293844626}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f2afce290bb870370d8ae6a0e683def4f1924fbf", "title": "Signals and Queries: Token-Efficient Agentic Context Discovery", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29326, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2348543832", "name": "Yam Marcovitz"}], "abstract": "User-facing LLM agents must identify which behavioral policies apply at each turn, but evaluating every policy with an LLM is costly. We introduce Signals and Queries (S&Q), a lightweight first-stage relevance filter developed for Parlant 4.0 that removes at least 30% of candidate policies before downstream evaluation while preserving high recall. For each policy, an LLM generates activating examples and hard negatives offline. An independent logistic classifier trained on their embeddings then predicts relevance using embeddings and vector dot products, with no per-turn LLM calls. This shifts expensive relevance evaluation to a smaller candidate set, while allowing each policy's classifier to be updated independently. Evaluated against 9,144 LLM-judge relevance labels covering 254 $\\tau^2$-bench conversation prefixes and 36 airline policies, S&Q achieves higher precision than top-K and cosine-threshold retrieval at every evaluated recall level, with consistent results across two embedding providers. These findings support inexpensive, per-policy classification as a practical first stage for reducing downstream evaluation workload in customer-facing agents."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "39fbfb27515a932caa2ac9d9a723c1941b87786f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.29317", "CorpusId": 293844861}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/39fbfb27515a932caa2ac9d9a723c1941b87786f", "title": "SpatialBrain: Spatial Intelligence Growing from Experience", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29317, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2384803761", "name": "Yi-Fan Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2349578127", "name": "Zi-Yang Gong"}, {"authorId": "2305627512", "name": "Xiao-Jun Ye"}, {"authorId": "2337824749", "name": "Yi-Fei Liu"}, {"authorId": "2448886444", "name": "Dong-Gang Li"}, {"authorId": "2155797490", "name": "Xiao-Long Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2266469531", "name": "Zhi-Hang Zhong"}, {"authorId": "2321523200", "name": "Xue-Ting Yang"}], "abstract": "Spatial reasoning requires more than access to geometric tools: a reasoner must decide when direct observation is sufficient and when to reconstruct, measure, or change reference frames. We present SpatialBrain, a framework that retains experience about these decisions while keeping its vision-language backbone fixed. The key distinction is between evidence about a scene and experience about how to reason with that evidence. 3DFusionMemory fuses current-scene observations into queryable spatial relations. A Spatial Skill Library provides reusable tool procedures, and Spatial Cognition associates task conditions with experience about when and how to use memory and skills. Completed trajectories are abstracted into process records and consolidated between task blocks, allowing later decisions to draw on earlier execution without transferring scene coordinates or reference answers. Across five spatial-reasoning benchmarks, SpatialBrain improves on direct answers from the same backbone on four and ties on the fifth. Component comparisons, experience-coverage curves, and traced resource decisions characterize how the framework accumulates and uses these resources."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4ee3952f4d555501a40e0b1790582a867e76b1f9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.29560", "CorpusId": 293856918}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4ee3952f4d555501a40e0b1790582a867e76b1f9", "title": "From VLM Planners to Robotic Agents: Self-Evolving Harnesses for History-Dependent Manipulation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29560, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2300285175", "name": "Ting-Wen Du"}, {"authorId": "2379685854", "name": "Hao-Yu Guo"}, {"authorId": "2275644424", "name": "Han-Xue Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2511826594", "name": "Xuan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2273884693", "name": "Jun-Ting Dong"}, {"authorId": "2505022516", "name": "Chao Gao"}, {"authorId": "2334521542", "name": "Ming-Fei Han"}, {"authorId": "2269712891", "name": "Tao Lu"}, {"authorId": "2269716664", "name": "Mu-Lin Yu"}, {"authorId": "2257475525", "name": "Chun-Hua Shen"}, {"authorId": "2358622438", "name": "Xiao-Jun Chang"}, {"authorId": "2267313229", "name": "Zhi-Hui Li"}], "abstract": "Vision-language-action (VLA) models achieve strong performance across embodied manipulation tasks by unifying visual understanding, language conditioning, and action generation. However, long-horizon tasks can involve non-Markovian dependencies: successful decisions may require retaining task progress and past interaction outcomes, while partial observability and execution errors compound over time. Existing work has begun to address these challenges by augmenting foundation-model policies with memory and by introducing hierarchical architectures that separate high-level planning from low-level execution. We organize the high-level VLM as a robotic agent through an editable harness to maintain task context and improve planning through execution experience. We instantiate this approach as SERA (Self-Evolving Robotic Agent), with a fixed VLA executor providing low-level control. The harness maintains and updates task context using observations and execution feedback to guide subsequent planning. Self-evolution uses environment rollouts to propose and evaluate harness revisions, with the selected configuration fixed before held-out testing. On RoboMME, SERA achieves 51.38% overall success with autonomous selection, compared with 44.38% without self-evolution. Our exploration suggests that agentic planning is a promising direction for history-dependent robotic manipulation."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ef12b85b1632958199fc5fc10f5445787cf5c375", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.30042", "CorpusId": 293856715}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ef12b85b1632958199fc5fc10f5445787cf5c375", "title": "HGM-Dual: Lineage-Aware Guided Agent Harness Optimization for Long-Horizon Planning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30042, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2277299467", "name": "Sudipta Paul"}, {"authorId": "2003336037", "name": "Peng-Rui Quan"}, {"authorId": "2344642721", "name": "Aounon Kumar"}, {"authorId": "2261733814", "name": "Nicholas Tzou"}, {"authorId": "2283136782", "name": "Vivek Kulkarni"}, {"authorId": "1667831197", "name": "Srinivas Chappidi"}], "abstract": "Harness optimization, which improves an agent's prompts, tools, and workflows, is an increasingly effective way to enhance agents built on frozen or closed-source LLMs. Leading approaches such as the Darwin--Godel Machine and the Huxley--Godel Machine (HGM) rely on recursive self-improvement and were developed mainly for coding, where the competence needed to improve the agent coincides with the competence being improved. Planning tasks, in which an agent must produce a sequence of tool calls that jointly satisfies interacting constraints, break this alignment. There, open-ended self-modification repeatedly explores ineffective changes and consumes substantial evaluation budgets. We introduce HGM-Dual, a diagnostic-guided framework for budget-efficient harness evolution for planning agents. First, HGM-Dual represents agent evolution as lineage-aware context. This context records what each ancestor changed, which behaviors improved or regressed, and which modifications were ineffective, giving the editor explicit edit-outcome attribution instead of an unstructured archive of past agents. Second, HGM-Dual introduces error-conditioned dual-path child generation. For each selected parent, it generates a general child that preserves open-ended exploration and specialized children that target the harness components most likely responsible for the parent's highest-ranked constraint violations. HGM-Dual outperforms the strongest baseline by up to 14.45% relative improvement in case accuracy while matching HGM variants'final performance with 2x-4x fewer evaluation budget."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e7860c473cc24dd5c64c7d696f40138fefab569d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.30478", "CorpusId": 293893797}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e7860c473cc24dd5c64c7d696f40138fefab569d", "title": "Externalise the Memory: A Pre-Registered Study of Where an Embodied Agent's Experience Should Live", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30478, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2350120919", "name": "Cheng-Ying Tu"}], "abstract": "Should an embodied agent's experience be baked into its policy weights, or kept external and retrieved at inference? A memory-augmented teacher (a VLM that retrieves lessons, with their episode images, from its own past attempts) produces trajectories on a vision-conditioned reach task; from the same trajectories we distil LoRA student policies and evaluate them under matched protocols, including one that re-attaches the teacher's frozen retrieval buffer to a fixed set of student weights. We pre-registered the hypotheses, arms, tests, and analysis rules before training and before any p-value. Distilling the memory-augmented behaviour into weights beat an action-only control by +1 pp (n.s.); the registered test proves underpowered for its own minimum effect, so we withdraw - rather than refute - the memory-in-weights claim. Attaching retrieval to a fixed set of student weights added +34 pp (z=5.15), reaching the pooled memory-augmented teacher reference (49.3%); the pre-registered protocol contrast agrees (+23 pp, z=3.36); under a pre-registered auto-reset sensitivity count the two are +36 and +29 pp. An exploratory behavioural probe with pre-registered per-arm predictions is consistent with behavioural distillation transferring the marginal correction but not the conditional, situation-specific correction that retrieval supplies. The recipe that follows is to externalise the memory: in follow-up controls registered before they ran, the same frozen buffer lifts the base model with no adapter to 48% (sensitivity 48%) and the other three adapters from 19-26% to 53-55% (sensitivity 17-21% to 50-55%), and the adapter adds nothing detectable once the buffer is attached. On a second, harder task, the retrieval advantage points the same direction but falls short of significance (+6 pp, 95% CI [-4.5, +16.4]); exploratory diagnostics tie the attenuation to memory coverage."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "823bb7adb84bdde5b0e1af911bfcba266b567783", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27588", "CorpusId": 293809264}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/823bb7adb84bdde5b0e1af911bfcba266b567783", "title": "Beyond Experience: Knowledge-Guided Evolution for NPU Kernel Generation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27588, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2516247014", "name": "Xi Chen"}, {"authorId": "118558736", "name": "Ming Shen"}, {"authorId": "2340748347", "name": "Hao-Tian Yan"}, {"authorId": "2243688647", "name": "Ruo-Yu Sun"}, {"authorId": "2301313448", "name": "Tian-Jun Yao"}], "abstract": "LLM agents have become capable kernel engineers on mainstream GPU stacks, but they struggle on emerging accelerators such as Ascend NPUs, whose programming model is barely represented in pretraining data. Recent NPU agents address this gap largely through experience: they accumulate memory from their own generation and optimization trials and reuse it on later tasks. We argue that experience alone is not enough for kernel generation. An agent can only remember what it has tried, and it rarely tries a mechanism it does not know. As a result, such memory offers little help when a new operator calls for a different mechanism, and even familiar operators raise unseen situations as optimization proceeds. We present ASCENDEVOLVE, which grounds every step of an evolutionary kernel search in a large external knowledge base built from platform documentation, toolkit headers, and operator repositories. Because this corpus far exceeds any context window, ASCENDEVOLVE indexes it and routes each agent to a small, task-specific slice of evidence instead of the corpus itself. The retrieved knowledge seeds diverse implementation strategies, guides repair when a candidate fails, and constrains how strong candidates are recombined. On 53 Ascend C operators from CANN-Bench spanning four difficulty levels, ASCENDEVOLVE achieves an overall score of 71.39, improving over Reflexion and OpenEvolve by 11.6% and 17.2%."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6f4a697361f728bebfe528c5cec7887bc7244dfd", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27693", "CorpusId": 293809743}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6f4a697361f728bebfe528c5cec7887bc7244dfd", "title": "Knowledge as Code", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27693, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2358259621", "name": "Martin Monperrus"}], "abstract": "For millennia, knowledge has been carried by language, and large language models still put language at the center. Yet the coding agents built on these models mostly write code. This paper is a contribution to epistemology, which asks what kind of knowledge code is and how it is obtained. We define knowledge as code as knowledge written in a programming language or in a machine-parsable data format, whose validity can be checked by executing it. We define thirteen epistemic properties of knowledge media and show that code is the first medium to have all of them. We distinguish two ways in which knowledge enters code, encoding and learning by execution, where code is both the experiment and the record of its result. Agents make learning by execution observable, since their trajectories record every probe, observation and line of code they keep. Code is thus not only a tool of knowledge work but a knowledge medium in its own right, the first that can check what it holds."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c2ca147e72da9a3e963067b0002ef6af40650517", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27610", "CorpusId": 293809462}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c2ca147e72da9a3e963067b0002ef6af40650517", "title": "Auditing the Product, Not the Recursion: The Evaluation Evidence Behind Recursive Self-Improvement Claims", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27610, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2255142697", "name": "Zi-Han Chen"}, {"authorId": "36737152", "name": "Di Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2233239462", "name": "L. Zheng"}], "abstract": "Recursive self-improvement (RSI) promises AI systems, such as self-evolving LLM agents, that become better at producing further improvements. Evaluating this promise requires separating product claims about the final system from process claims about the improvement process itself. Existing surveys specify the evidence these claims require, but how often studies report that evidence remains unmeasured. We examine this question through full-text coding of 114 eligible studies in a reproducible arXiv search frame frozen in October 2026, including 90 that describe their own systems as RSI. The codebook links claims to evaluation designs. Independent human coding, third-reader adjudication, and a separate blind coding of improver comparisons assess coding reliability. Among the 90 RSI studies, 90% report product evaluation beyond development tasks under a broad criterion that includes partial or unstated separation, while 60% report evaluation on tasks unused for selection. Only 22% audit two or more reruns of the full loop. In a human-coded random sample, the RSI studies show the same contrast: 45% evaluate on tasks unused for selection and 14% audit two or more reruns. Of the 26 studies claiming a better improvement procedure, 23 report a related comparison, but only 4 compare earlier and later procedures from identical starting artifacts at a matched budget. Across these results and a comparison set of established works, evidence for repeatability and improved procedures remains less developed than product evaluation. Three propositions explain how selection effects, shared evaluator errors, and changes in system state limit what product evaluations can establish. Together, the findings support a claim-indexed reporting standard that connects each claim to the evaluation design needed to test it."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0de90f8c4f431797dd9234e93d40c7ec85d3e285", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27714", "CorpusId": 293810067}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0de90f8c4f431797dd9234e93d40c7ec85d3e285", "title": "When Does Experience Memory Help Closed-Loop LLM Driving Agents? A Controlled Evaluation with Resampled Warmup Pools, Token-Matched Fillers, and Audited Baselines", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27714, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2516239785", "name": "Ying-Xin Jia"}, {"authorId": "2504354514", "name": "Xuan-Yi Li"}], "abstract": "When does experience memory help a closed-loop LLM driving agent? Published gains cannot say, because the two factors we find to matter most, which warmup experiences the pool contains and what any injected tokens do to the prompt, are controlled in none of them. We answer with a controlled evaluation on one audited HighwayEnv testbed (1Hz, gpt-4o-mini for the decisive contrasts). We treat the warmup pool as a resampled random factor (ten disjoint pools) and add token-matched irrelevant-filler controls, oracle-certified diagnostics, caution-pricing metrics, and quantified audits of the baseline ports. The probe suite spans flat-text retrieval (a DiLu port), a single-frame grid port (RESPOND-inspired), our episodic temporal-graph memory, a retrieval-free consolidated rulebook, and a flat K-step observation-history baseline. Four findings follow, each a property of this testbed. (1) Headline memory gains belong to the warmup draw. The reported pool is the best of ten for both retrieval memories, which beat No-Memory in only one pool of ten (sign test p=0.011), and even the rulebook's apparent pool-immunity collapsed on five fresh draws. (2) No retrieval design tested exceeds token-matched filler. The collision-only merge ceiling is reached by a single 15-token caution instruction, and metrics that price caution erase the rulebook's lead. (3) Representational sophistication is nowhere robustly operative. (4) Baseline port quality silently inverts conclusions. On this testbed, a single-pool comparison without filler controls would have reported memory gains that these controls reverse. We release the testbed, audited ports, seeds, and caches."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "bc31aa213525efa8ae6c719200d5b0c497300a4f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.28386", "CorpusId": 293811998}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bc31aa213525efa8ae6c719200d5b0c497300a4f", "title": "VisualEvolve: A Skill Self-Evolution Framework for Visual Reasoning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28386, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2506267525", "name": "Jia-Wei Wang"}, {"authorId": "2516246733", "name": "Yu-Cun Li"}, {"authorId": "2525167378", "name": "Xue-Qi Li"}, {"authorId": "2516243744", "name": "Sheng-Hao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2516055551", "name": "Xin-Quan Gong"}, {"authorId": "2266277013", "name": "Dan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2382965039", "name": "Zhe-Qi Lv"}, {"authorId": "2258285591", "name": "Yu-Quan Le"}, {"authorId": "1429199889", "name": "Shao-Zu Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2311074323", "name": "An Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2147412314", "name": "Da Cao"}, {"authorId": "2351809900", "name": "Tat-Seng Chua"}], "abstract": "Visual tool use enables vision-language models (VLMs) to acquire task-relevant evidence, but its effectiveness can be limited by predefined visual operations and the tool-use strategies learned during training. Representing these operations and strategies as reusable external skills allows them to be refined through self-evolution without updating model parameters. However, skill refinement can introduce performance regressions. Meanwhile, inspecting accumulated task execution trajectories together with skill evolution history may lead to evolution evidence overload. To address these challenges, we propose VisualEvolve, a self-evolving framework that evolves executable visual operations and tool-use strategies for VLMs through a Select--Execute--Evolve--Evaluate cycle. Skill-Library Snapshot Selection uses performance on a fixed validation set disjoint from execution tasks to select an effective historical snapshot for subsequent evolution, limiting the propagation of performance regressions. Progressive Evidence Access (PEA) guides capability-gap diagnosis through a coarse-to-fine hierarchy comprising the Skill-Performance Report, Evolution Log, Trajectory Summaries, and relevant raw trajectory segments. Across four VLM backbones and six evaluation sets from three benchmarks, VisualEvolve improves over the Initial Skill Library in 23 of 24 settings, with a maximum gain of 15.1 percentage points on VisualProbe Hard using Qwen3.5-Plus. Ablation studies support the contributions of Snapshot Selection and PEA, while further experiments show that skills can be improved using experience from different data settings and directly reused across backbones. Moreover, VisualEvolve enables effective skill self-evolution with over an order of magnitude fewer task-solving trajectories than estimated for representative RL-based methods."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ea5ad53bb05b9e41faf7fe19ea53d94b23a9d413", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.28069", "CorpusId": 293812221}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ea5ad53bb05b9e41faf7fe19ea53d94b23a9d413", "title": "Training the Whole Agent, Not Just the Model", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28069, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2266119899", "name": "Ling-Xiao Tang"}, {"authorId": "2112528845", "name": "Wei Tao"}, {"authorId": "2318303849", "name": "Yi-Wen Guo"}, {"authorId": "2385724318", "name": "Ling-Feng Bao"}], "abstract": "Training coding agents typically updates the policy while keeping the harness fixed. During training, trajectories reveal flawed repair strategies and interaction problems that vary across tasks and change as the policy learns. We introduce HarnessAdapt-RL, which trains the policy and adapts its harness during reinforcement learning. For flawed repair strategies, a frozen teacher generates task-specific mechanism-level skills. For interaction problems recurring across tasks, a separate teacher model evolves tools and hooks, keeping each change only if it improves the observed pass rate or the observed trainable rate without lowering either. The policy trains with the adapted harness, and its new trajectories guide further adaptation. With a 9B base model, HarnessAdapt-RL improves pass@1 over GRPO by 9.8, 12.6, and 6.4 percentage points on SWE-bench Verified, Multilingual, and Pro, and outperforms GRPO with the human-designed OpenHands harness. Gains from mechanism-level skills persist after their removal at evaluation. Re-evolving tools and hooks as the policy changes outperforms freezing early versions. Removing them at deployment, in contrast, costs up to 7.7 percentage points. Training therefore produces a model-harness pair: the trained policy and the evolved tools and hooks, with skills removed at deployment."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "35cb61680cb6be63f332956da8b9b66d17fe7981", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.28914", "CorpusId": 293844720}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/35cb61680cb6be63f332956da8b9b66d17fe7981", "title": "D-Skill: Distilling Teacher Knowledge through Agent Skills", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28914, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2379614165", "name": "Shuo Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2265517766", "name": "Da-Cheng Li"}, {"authorId": "2346365389", "name": "Joseph Gonzalez"}], "abstract": "Frontier language-model agents excel at complex tasks, but high inference costs make transferring their capabilities to smaller models essential. Traditional knowledge distillation achieves this through parameter updates, requiring access to the student's weights and additional training compute while encoding the transferred knowledge implicitly in the parameters. We introduce D-Skill, a framework that treats the teacher as a black box and distills its behavioral advantage into an interpretable natural-language skill by analyzing paired rollouts and identifying behavioral divergences and agreements. The resulting lessons are consolidated offline, with any compatible skill-update backend, into a skill document that is provided to the student at inference time. D-Skill constructs the skill entirely offline from fixed trajectories, without labels, further environment interaction, or weight updates after rollout collection. Across SpreadsheetBench-Verified, ALFWorld, and chess puzzles spanning multiple teacher-student pairs, D-Skill closes 75%-95% of the teacher-student gap without touching model weights. After a single offline pass over the paired traces, D-Skill outperforms one-epoch SFT baselines and remains competitive with multi-epoch SFT. Under the same model family, the resulting student also incurs 5.7x-8.3x lower inference cost per task than the teacher. Our results demonstrate that natural-language skill distillation offers a performant, interpretable, and efficient alternative to weight-based capability transfer."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "57b1b31d132f312efdda6f096bd507ee18971849", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.28892", "CorpusId": 293844791}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/57b1b31d132f312efdda6f096bd507ee18971849", "title": "Combating Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Domain Adaptation via Knowledge Recasting", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28892, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2342746625", "name": "Zi-Jie Liu"}, {"authorId": "2354228808", "name": "Zhen Tan"}, {"authorId": "2394170606", "name": "Charles Fleming"}, {"authorId": "2353158386", "name": "Tian-Long Chen"}], "abstract": "Adapting language models to new domains during post-training risks degrading previously acquired capabilities, a phenomenon commonly known as catastrophic forgetting. Existing mitigation methods largely treat forgetting as a consequence of excessive model drift. They therefore constrain updates by preserving old outputs, replaying old data, or regularizing changes to parameters and representations so that the updated model remains close to its previous state. We take a different view: Rather than pulling the updated model back toward its old state, we translate the new domain into the model's pre-existing knowledge space, making unfamiliar knowledge learnable through familiar procedures. Therefore, we propose Skill Schema Transport (SST), a post-training framework that transports new knowledge into the model's existing skill space. SST abstracts each example type into a skill schema, preserving its essential operations, dependencies, and verification steps while removing domain-specific surface forms. By recasting new examples as instances of reusable procedures, SST turns knowledge update from trajectory memorization into procedural reuse. In continual domain adaptation settings, SST consistently reduces forgetting while improving generalization to related tasks. Across two 3B instruction-tuned backbones, SST improves average final performance on streamed domains by 2.53 to 5.75 points over sequential post-training. It also lowers max-drop forgetting in all evaluated settings, with the largest reduction from 42.41 to 7.10 points on the Qwen-3B MATH stream, while substantially recovering the OOD degradation induced by sequential updates."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4dc7ccb274f778815f8ccf0166f0044be0771851", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.26453", "CorpusId": 293800713}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4dc7ccb274f778815f8ccf0166f0044be0771851", "title": "Learning What to Remember: Resource-Efficient Self-Improvement for Lifelong Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.26453, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2339270147", "name": "Guannan Lai"}, {"authorId": "2515835290", "name": "Zi-Yang Bai"}, {"authorId": "2304704645", "name": "Jun-Peng Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2304399570", "name": "Han-Jia Ye"}], "abstract": "Self-improving agents aim to continuously improve their behavior by learning from experience after deployment. However, accumulating and reusing more experience does not necessarily lead to better decisions, particularly when generation, interaction, and memory resources are jointly constrained. Under such stringent constraints, we find that several existing lifelong-memory methods provide limited benefits over simple retrieval or even no-memory agents. This reveals a fundamental challenge for resource-efficient self-improvement: agents must learn not only which past experiences are relevant, but which are actually worth reusing to improve future decisions. When memory capacity and opportunities for reasoning and recovery are limited, retrieving an ineffective experience can consume scarce resources and hinder downstream execution. To address this challenge, we propose TokenWorth, a lightweight feedback-driven memory adaptation framework that learns query-dependent memory worth from sparse execution feedback. Without updating the backbone model, TokenWorth uses the learned worth to guide both experience retrieval and bounded memory management, enabling the agent to continually adapt how it reuses accumulated experience. Across three LifelongAgentBench environments and both local and API-based deployments, TokenWorth achieves the highest task success rate while using the fewest output tokens among the evaluated methods under the specified resource budgets. These results suggest that effective self-improvement under resource constraints depends not on accumulating more experience, but on learning what is worth remembering. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/AIGNLAI/TokenWorth."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e14d9852fb968ade7b06e88199ff27ec54aeec5a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.25959", "CorpusId": 293802677}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e14d9852fb968ade7b06e88199ff27ec54aeec5a", "title": "EmbodiedWorld-200K: Toward Embodied World Agents via Embodied-Planning Dataset and Interactive World Models", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25959, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2515984635", "name": "X. Feng"}, {"authorId": "2516004231", "name": "J. Tang"}, {"authorId": "2515978844", "name": "Z. Lin"}, {"authorId": "2265722184", "name": "L. Chen"}, {"authorId": "2362456241", "name": "S. Shao"}, {"authorId": "2516002638", "name": "Q. Lu"}, {"authorId": "2515985584", "name": "K. Huang"}], "abstract": "Building embodied agents that can perceive, reason, and act across diverse 3D worlds is a central research direction toward artificial general intelligence. Yet most existing work remains confined to specific tasks in a single scene and transfers poorly to new environments, leaving general embodied agents for open-world settings under-explored. We refer to such agents as Embodied World Agents (EWAs), whose defining property is the ability to understand diverse visual environments and execute general actions in open-world conditions. To advance EWA research, we study open-world embodied navigation-planning and release EmbodiedWorld-200K, a large-scale dataset of over 200K samples spanning diverse scenes. Each example is equipped with multi-level annotations, ranging from low-level camera-motion trajectories to high-level semantic instructions. The dataset also provides unified evaluation splits and task-specific metrics, supporting offline pretraining and fair evaluation of EWAs. Beyond offline supervision, a defining characteristic of an EWA is its interaction with the environment; training solely on offline data is therefore insufficient, especially for generalizing to scenes not covered in the training distribution. Building dedicated real interactive environments, however, is often prohibitively expensive. To address this, we propose a novel training scheme that employs a video world model as a low-cost environment proxy and performs interactive reinforcement-learning (RL) post-training of the EWA within it. Empirically, pretraining on EmbodiedWorld-200K substantially improves the open-world embodied planning ability of the EWA, and further RL post-training inside a world model yields clear gains on out-of-distribution scenes. The dataset, baseline checkpoints, and training and evaluation code are available at https://xiaokunfeng.github.io/EmbodiedWorld-200K/."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "aa95c9da30f2cf0c9aff67f56a8cb00f02cc2d4e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.25931", "CorpusId": 293800030}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/aa95c9da30f2cf0c9aff67f56a8cb00f02cc2d4e", "title": "EvoSkill v2: Fully Agentic Skill Evolution from Benchmark Adaptation to a Stateful Evolver", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25931, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2505037074", "name": "Da-Wei Huang"}, {"authorId": "2408455839", "name": "Baran Nama"}, {"authorId": "2186553606", "name": "Salaheddin Alzubi"}], "abstract": "EvoSkill v2 is a three-agent system that edits the instructions (skills) an agent is given, leaving its model and harness intact. The user starts a run by describing a benchmark and supplying a grader, which a benchmark adaptor turns into a benchmark-specific plugin. A solver attempts the tasks, and an evolver reads the solver's training rollouts and rewrites the skills at each epoch. We implement the evolver as one stateful coding agent with a bash tool for the whole run, unlike the two most common designs among the skill optimizers we surveyed: a sequence of single model calls, as in SkillOpt, and an agent that starts afresh each round, as in SkillBoost. We also measure how much the solver's rollouts vary from one attempt to the next, give the evolver several attempts per training task, and accept every epoch's skills instead of selecting among them on noisy scores. With fine-grained ground truth, EvoSkill v2's skills raised the held-out pass rate above the stronger of SkillOpt and SkillBoost by 7.7 percentage points on SpreadsheetBench v2 and by 5.4 on $\\tau^3$-bench banking. In both benchmarks and both feedback settings, EvoSkill v2's evolver cost less per percentage point of held-out pass rate gained than either baseline's optimizer. EvoSkill v2 also enables a less capable model to improve its own skills, demonstrated by a gain of up to 21.5 percentage points in the $\\tau^3$-bench banking experiment where Claude Haiku 5.5 powers both solver and evolver."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "5b3933cddde122f8828cceef16bd04527d28dfec", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.25985", "CorpusId": 293800915}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5b3933cddde122f8828cceef16bd04527d28dfec", "title": "NIOCIS: Typed-Decision Next-Hop Routing with Node-Conditioned Option Spaces for Tool-Use Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25985, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2524814915", "name": "Jin-Sung Han"}, {"authorId": "2037488623", "name": "Ming-Feng Xin"}, {"authorId": "2399164305", "name": "Feng Liang"}, {"authorId": "2515997878", "name": "J. Yu"}], "abstract": "Tool-use agents pay per decision: at each hop a language model re-reads the state and thinks for seconds, though its actions are finite and decisions recur. We present NIOCIS, a routing layer that folds execution logs into a loop-collapsed action graph (forward transitions plus recovery back-edges) answering one typed question: which declared option at a state, plus stop, does the fleet take next? A small model reads a calibrated distribution over them in one pass, emitting no text. On 1,615 production security-operations episodes we measure what the layer replaces, returns and is bounded by. Replaces: the typed encoder matches the logged next hop on 32.2% of the 3,875 held-out decisions (34.9% within the declared options) in 10 ms of encoder time (19.5 ms for the typed-choice form), against 2.2 s per LLM hop; two feature readouts reach 32.8% and 32.3%, an ordering the paired test does not resolve, so the typed layer is chosen for cost, not a separated lead. Tuning: jpt-4b reaches 9.9% zero-shot and 31.9% fine-tuned, a gap an exact paired test does not resolve (p=0.68), at 244 ms. Returns little: the realised hop falls outside the declared options for 7.9% of decisions and outside the model's top twenty for 9.3%; the most confident 15% holds 91.9% accuracy. Bounded: a cross-trace leave-one-out test puts the released state's ceiling at 25.6--44.9% as the state key grows more specific, i.e. repeated states disagree more often than they agree; the option-space ablation attributes the gain to per-node candidate restriction (+3.5 points over a flat catalog) rather than graph traversal (zero within its interval); a gated self-evolution loop lifts every arm above its anchor yet ends below full supervision; a confidence-gated cascade loses agreement when the escalation arm is the zero-shot stand-in; and agreement past a third of the fleet needs a state carrying the plan and the observation."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "664395f966d458896bc6ecf2e279689e48cee88b", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.26848", "CorpusId": 293804891}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/664395f966d458896bc6ecf2e279689e48cee88b", "title": "Where is Physical RSI capability from? An Observational Scaling Law of LLM-as-policy", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.26848, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2457303785", "name": "Ze-Kai Li"}, {"authorId": "2515999943", "name": "Jia-Yi Yu"}, {"authorId": "2506270318", "name": "Jin-Lu Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2382919712", "name": "Lu-Yang Niu"}, {"authorId": "2385150703", "name": "Ze-Kun Qi"}, {"authorId": "2501918775", "name": "Jun-Yang Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2265920777", "name": "Xu-Chuan Chen"}, {"authorId": "2301193049", "name": "Dai-En Liu"}, {"authorId": "2440685250", "name": "Yintianrun Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2336953381", "name": "He Wang"}, {"authorId": "2242612318", "name": "Li Yi"}], "abstract": "In this paper, we systematically investigate the mechanisms, scaling behavior, and generalization of physical Recursive self-improvement. We introduce PhysRSI-eval to quantify physical RSI through a five-axis metric: First-Attempt Performance, Efficiency, Success, Budget and Improvement from failure. Different VLMs are evaluated on 18 manipulation tasks under a fixed interaction budget. The analysis of experimental results motivates the Physical Barrel Hypothesis: different capabilities may constrain different parts of improvement. The five-axis metrics also distinguish systems with close public benchmark averages and show that high scores need not come with early or efficient gains. We study observational scaling from two perspectives: predicting the relative performance of VLM agents from their public capabilities, and forecasting later progress from early physical feedback. Together, these analyses ask how quickly and how far an agent can improve. Moreover, we conduct workspace interventions and then analyze what improvement leaves behind. Removing history reduces late-round success, frozen workspaces remain useful on unseen layouts, and three cross-task cases achieve success earlier. The results suggest that scene-specific adaptation and reusable experience can coexist."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "700a5af40b25a294dae6381d71a7c41d120faa4e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.26098", "CorpusId": 293807466}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/700a5af40b25a294dae6381d71a7c41d120faa4e", "title": "From Code to Playable Systems: CEGame-Bench for Long-Horizon Game Creation Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.26098, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2304549951", "name": "Zi-Xuan Dong"}, {"authorId": "2377579105", "name": "Yang-Hai Wang"}, {"authorId": "2315540800", "name": "Han-Yan Bian"}, {"authorId": "2503712639", "name": "Ji Tao"}, {"authorId": "2299106618", "name": "Jun-Ze Li"}, {"authorId": "2515993517", "name": "Jia-Dong Wang"}, {"authorId": "2408464177", "name": "Xin-Yu Che"}, {"authorId": "2515997552", "name": "Lin Liu"}, {"authorId": "2304423465", "name": "Bao-Yun Peng"}, {"authorId": "2363498987", "name": "Xiao-Dong Wang"}, {"authorId": "2346051109", "name": "Jia-Heng Liu"}], "abstract": "A game ships when a player can launch it, finish it, and return to a saved world, none of which a successful build guarantees, yet game-creation agents are mostly tested on one engine or on one-prompt browser games. We present CEGame-Bench, the first long-horizon benchmark for complete-game creation across five production engines: Defold, Godot, Roblox Studio, Unity, and Unreal Engine. Agents build each game from an empty project and a design document in runs averaging up to 295 steps and 65M tokens; the document doubles as a contract, and a hidden evaluator accepts a game only by real-input play. Under four scaffolds, agents earn most integration points but usually under half the gameplay points, and their games reach the ending in only 16% of runs, while references built by engineers with coding agents score near the maximum, which places this artifact-to-play gap in the agents. No two engines rank configurations alike (Kendall's tau 0.14-0.62), and the same game usually scores higher on Unity and Unreal Engine, which check more before play. We release Game Completion Boost, an agent skill for requirement-driven playtesting and repair that raises the execution-based score of three models by 14.1-24.3 points."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7b75807f8aac9b83b41d5df0216fd2fafb376690", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27406", "CorpusId": 293809106}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7b75807f8aac9b83b41d5df0216fd2fafb376690", "title": "More Memory, Worse Agents: Error Reproduction and Anti-Persistence in LLM Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27406, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2328617416", "name": "Ru-Han Wang"}, {"authorId": "1518267866", "name": "Kishan Panaganti"}, {"authorId": "2292161506", "name": "Dong-Ruo Zhou"}], "abstract": "LLM agents can improve across tasks without updating model parameters by storing experience from their own executions in external memory and reusing it to guide later decisions. A central design question is whether to retain only observed trajectories and outcomes or also the rules, summaries, and skills inferred from them. Retaining these abstractions allows agents to reuse strategies without repeatedly deriving them from raw trajectories. However, errors in stored abstractions can propagate to subsequent decisions through retrieval and reuse. They can also shape execution trajectories, which then become the basis for new abstractions that reproduce the original errors. We term this feedback mechanism error reproduction: errors are regenerated through a cycle of abstraction, execution, and re-abstraction, extending their persistence beyond direct reuse of the original faulty knowledge. We propose Anti-Persistence, a memory design that accumulates trajectories and outcomes while reconstructing task-specific guidance on demand. An adaptive selector uses prior outcomes to decide whether and how to synthesize guidance, which is discarded after execution. This prevents its direct reuse as a stored object, although indirect feedback through retained trajectories remains possible. Across different benchmarks and backbones, Anti-Persistence achieves higher overall held-out success and the lowest average LLM-call cost among the evaluated memory designs. These results support separating experience accumulation from abstraction persistence in memorybased self-improvement."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d5f66db7accd06cdb3bd07f3935257285e1ee2a3", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27227", "CorpusId": 293809978}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d5f66db7accd06cdb3bd07f3935257285e1ee2a3", "title": "Which Agent's Skill Should Change? Group-Relative Credit Assignment for Evolving Multi-Agent Teams", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27227, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2291054660", "name": "Ya-Nan Ma"}, {"authorId": "2470772938", "name": "Yang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2386515532", "name": "David Le"}, {"authorId": "2316523439", "name": "Chen-Han Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2328724165", "name": "Yi-Zheng Sun"}, {"authorId": "2379686539", "name": "Guang-Wei Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2366694043", "name": "Cheng-Hao Xiao"}, {"authorId": "2279451646", "name": "Cheng-Hua Lin"}, {"authorId": "2505632305", "name": "Imon Banerjee"}], "abstract": "Cooperative language-model agents can improve without weight updates by revising reusable skills, but only if a shared team reward is traced to the responsible agent and skill. Existing methods credit the skills present in each trajectory, yet presence is not responsibility: a harmful skill active in successful runs still earns positive credit. We present \\textbf{GRSE}, \\textbf{G}roup-\\textbf{R}elative \\textbf{S}kill \\textbf{E}volution, which assigns behavioral credit by comparison: inspired by GRPO, it executes each question several times under the same skill libraries and contrasts the executions to credit agent--skill pairs, with structured diagnosis of failures. On HotpotQA and LoCoMo with two open-weight actors, GRSE outperforms MASkills, the closest prior method, on HotpotQA and every LoCoMo category except open-domain, with the largest gains on multi-hop questions ($+6.7$ and $+3.2$ F1). Its gains rest on better-grounded retrieval rather than answers given without evidence, and its learned skills transfer between the multi-hop tasks. GRSE is robust to corrupted libraries: starting from planted harmful skills, it credits every planted skill with the correct sign and recovers the score of a clean library ($+6.3$ points), whereas MASkills misattributes two and falls below its starting point."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8dc5714e012d22c33055df5d1172618a49d48860", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27327", "CorpusId": 293809358}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8dc5714e012d22c33055df5d1172618a49d48860", "title": "Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27327, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2145435513", "name": "Yuqing Yang"}, {"authorId": "2262420822", "name": "Yurong Liu"}, {"authorId": "2135593484", "name": "Deqing Fu"}, {"authorId": "2055805642", "name": "Manav Garg"}, {"authorId": "40696276", "name": "S. Bodapati"}, {"authorId": "2324057763", "name": "Fatma Ozcan"}, {"authorId": "1770962", "name": "A. Halevy"}, {"authorId": "2258747730", "name": "Jia-Ni Zhang"}], "abstract": "Coding agents can answer analytical questions over a data lake by discovering, cleaning, and joining the relevant data. However, they discard this work after each question and redo it from scratch for the next one. Tool evolution, which automatically builds and refines reusable tools across tasks, is a natural fit for this repetition. Since a ground-truth label for a data-lake question requires an expert to solve it first, we consider the unsupervised setting. We propose TEAL (Tool Evolution for Analytics over data Lakes), which evolves a library of tools and usage skills for a given Worker agent without labels. Two agents propose tools from complementary evidence, an Explorer from the data lake itself and a Critic from Worker trajectories. A proposed change is kept only if the Worker adopts the tool on a rerun without extra steps and any changed answer is supported by trajectory evidence. We also introduce TEAL-Q, which generates synthetic questions for tool evolution when real ones are unavailable. We evaluate on KramaBench and LakeQA with four Worker LLMs under step budgets, which cap the number of steps the Worker may take per question. With a tight budget of 10 or 15 steps, TEAL improves every Worker on both benchmarks, lifting Sonnet-5 at 15 steps by 9.3 points on KramaBench and 21.7 points on LakeQA. A tighter budget also yields better feedback for evolution. For example, Sonnet-5's LakeQA library evolved at 20 steps and deployed unchanged at 30 steps scores 68.3, above both the baseline (59.6) and a library evolved directly at 30 steps (64.2). Additionally, a library evolved on synthetic questions performs comparably to one evolved on real questions. These results show that useful tools can be evolved without labels, and hopefully inspire future work on what to evolve and how to obtain feedback in unsupervised agent evolution."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d0d8083d90a4990d37ff04ef95ba160907412019", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27377", "CorpusId": 293809761}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d0d8083d90a4990d37ff04ef95ba160907412019", "title": "REM: What to Store, When to Recall, What to Forget in Experience Memory for Coding Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27377, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2510546322", "name": "Xin-You Chen"}, {"authorId": "2201067308", "name": "Pedro Herrero-Vidal"}, {"authorId": "2499363552", "name": "Xiao-Han Chen"}, {"authorId": "2276527790", "name": "Xiao-Jing Yu"}, {"authorId": "2324679735", "name": "Cris Liu"}, {"authorId": "2266173370", "name": "Bing-Qing Song"}, {"authorId": "2516258861", "name": "Bin Xu"}, {"authorId": "2265719701", "name": "Suleiman A. Khan"}, {"authorId": "2294390078", "name": "You Chen"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents now autonomously complete long-horizon tasks, such as repository-level coding, through hundreds of think-act steps, yet the experience each run produces is rarely reused on the next. Prior memory systems concentrate on retrieval, finding the most relevant past trajectory. Retrieval cannot observe the running agent, so it cannot decide whether the agent needs an experience, at which point, or for how long. We introduce REM (Reusable Experience Memory), a framework that manages the memory lifecycle from inside the agent's think-act loop through three explicit decisions. What to store: each completed trajectory is consolidated into a phase-structured document, two orders of magnitude smaller than the trajectory. When to recall: the document is injected at the start of the task; the costlier implementation record is withheld until an in-loop gate detects the agent struggling. What to forget: the record is evicted after a single exposure. On repository-level bug fixing (SWE-ContextBench Lite), REM retrieves the related trajectory more accurately than every state-of-the-art memory system (68.7% vs. 55.6% match@1) and improves, in every replicate, end-task resolution (27.6% vs. 24.9%) at 17% lower cost. Injecting the same experience as a summary or raw trajectory, even when oracle-retrieved, does not. Ablating any of the three decisions reduces resolution. Altogether, these results demonstrate that the bottleneck in experience reuse is not retrieval fidelity but how experience is stored and governed across its lifecycle, opening a control-theoretic perspective on agent memory."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "bdc6c6174d06daf87116aed0e208e9760e2cf6d3", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.1002/gamm.70016", "CorpusId": 293827370}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bdc6c6174d06daf87116aed0e208e9760e2cf6d3", "title": "Agentic artificial intelligence in mechanics of materials: A review of building tailored models on demand", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdfdirect/10.1002/gamm.70016", "status": "HYBRID", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1002/gamm.70016?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1002/gamm.70016, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2332536882", "name": "M. Tacke"}, {"authorId": "2332536211", "name": "Matthias Busch"}, {"authorId": "2364310856", "name": "Kevin Linka"}, {"authorId": "2364117705", "name": "Roland C. Aydin"}, {"authorId": "2391259767", "name": "Christian J. Cyron"}], "abstract": "\n \n In mechanics of materials, many powerful methods are accessible only to experts. Constitutive models, for example, describe the relation between stress and strain in materials and are a core ingredient of simulations from structural engineering to biomechanics. Yet building and using such models requires specialized expertise\u2014a barrier for the many potential users trained in other fields. Artificial intelligence based on foundation models, increasingly organized as agents, is beginning to lower this barrier by allowing users to generate tailored models on demand. The same development is underway in many other fields where specialized tools have long been hand\u2010crafted anew for each task. We call this emerging paradigm the\n on\u2010the\u2010fly generation of physical models\n : from a plain request, an agent creates a physical model on demand\u2014from scratch and tailored to the problem. Its appeal is accessibility and flexibility. As a consequence, the lasting artifact is no longer a fixed model but the orchestration and prompting of the agents that produce it. This review surveys the concepts behind the paradigm, within mechanics of materials and in neighboring fields, and examines them through two lenses: effectiveness (the accuracy of the generated models) and trustworthiness (their physical admissibility). Trustworthiness is pursued along two routes: deepening the agent's integration under explicit validation, or restraining its autonomy in favor of trusted components. Given the rapid progress of foundation models, and the bitter lesson that general methods leveraging computation tend to prevail, we call for paradigms designed to scale with this progress rather than to compete with it.\n"}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "010cde7453048f2bbaa30eeb2fb3029661968536", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.1093/9780197840375.001.0001", "CorpusId": 293865503}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/010cde7453048f2bbaa30eeb2fb3029661968536", "title": "AI Welfare", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": "CLOSED", "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1093/9780197840375.001.0001?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1093/9780197840375.001.0001, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2286079241", "name": "Simon Goldstein"}, {"authorId": "1423334939", "name": "Cameron Domenico Kirk-Giannini"}], "abstract": "\n This book systematically investigates the possibility of artificial intelligence (AI) welfare. It focuses on three plausible sufficient conditions for welfare: having beliefs and desires, being conscious, and feeling pleasure and displeasure. The book explores the leading philosophical theories of each condition and applies them to AIs. It argues that some existing AIs plausibly have beliefs and desires; that some existing AIs could plausibly be modified in small ways to become conscious; and that if these systems could be made conscious, they could easily be made to feel pleasure and displeasure. This constitutes a provisional case that AIs already or soon may have welfare. In addition, the book lays out nine open questions about AI welfare, as follows: How should one identify which desires contribute to welfare? Is consciousness required for welfare? Do simple AIs like Roombas have welfare? Do tiny neural networks satisfy the conditions of functionalist theories of consciousness? Does consciousness require rich representational formats? Could AIs experience bodily pleasures and displeasures? Are pleasure and displeasure essentially access conscious? Are simulated minds real? Are LLMs and related systems merely stochastic parrots? The book lays out provisional answers to each of these questions, while also leaving room for future discussion."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "471b5fd6bf1dc0c9836d70cbc84ebf31ff7b0a8c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.25455", "CorpusId": 293805701}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/471b5fd6bf1dc0c9836d70cbc84ebf31ff7b0a8c", "title": "Self-Crystallizing Behavior Trees: Amortizing Foundation-Model Reasoning into Deterministic Control for Autonomous Drones", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25455, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-18", "authors": [{"authorId": "88969241", "name": "Behnam Moradi"}], "abstract": "Classical robot control, Finite State Machines and Behavior Trees (BTs), is deterministic and inspectable but fixed at design time: it fails on situations its designers did not anticipate. Foundation models can synthesize new control logic at runtime, at a cost in latency and non-determinism hard to accept in safety-critical aerial systems. Existing LLM-driven approaches keep the model in the loop permanently and discard what it produces. We propose Self-Crystallizing Behavior Trees (SCBT), in which the foundation model is a compiler rather than a runtime. When a BT node fails, an LLM proposes a minimal, mission-agnostic subtree patch; CMA-ES tunes its parameters in simulation; a contextual bandit decides whether a stored patch or a new LLM call is worth its cost; and a three-stage admission pipeline (static check, invariant-monitored replays, an empirical margin over the unpatched tree) admits only patches that demonstrably recover. Admitted patches are stored in a fleet library and, once the LLM call rate decays, crystallized into a deterministic BT that no human wrote. We implement SCBT on Microsoft Project AirSim with a schema-constrained LLM pipeline (Gemini 3.1 Pro) and a lockstep simulator. Over three seeds of a 20-episode learning phase with wind-gust and battery failures, SCBT used $11\\pm1.4$ LLM calls against 26 for whole-tree regeneration and 22 without a library, with 0.33 calls per episode in the second half. The crystallized trees, 28 and 33 nodes with two patches each, reached $97\\pm2\\%$ mission success with no learning running, against 12\\% for the unpatched tree and 98\\% for a hand-coded expert tree, and reproduce decision traces exactly when re-ticked offline. Two negative results: replay admission rejected useful wind recoveries by attributing wind-induced geofence excursions to the patch, and a static-only ablation generalized better to held-out failures."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a0086d13e5ec9ae5f8ad0564e9dff6b8b97256bd", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.25374", "CorpusId": 293805216}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a0086d13e5ec9ae5f8ad0564e9dff6b8b97256bd", "title": "Mining the Self-Rewriting System: A Vision for Data Mining on AI-Native Infrastructure", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25374, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-18", "authors": [{"authorId": "2439954", "name": "Mayank Darbari"}, {"authorId": "51219074", "name": "Chiman Salavati"}, {"authorId": "2264480350", "name": "Liang Wu"}, {"authorId": "2515994405", "name": "Da-Wei Wang"}, {"authorId": "2515992458", "name": "Jiong Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2319117545", "name": "Kelly Wan"}, {"authorId": "2099634416", "name": "Krishna Chakka"}, {"authorId": "2264620213", "name": "Liang-Jie Hong"}], "abstract": "Classical data mining treats data as generated outside the learner. Emerging AI-native infrastructure invalidates this assumption: as agents observe system state, act, update retrieval stores and models, and promote new artifacts into the serving path, their own decisions reshape the future logs, traces, corpora, and evaluation records used for later training and release. We call this new research object a self-rewriting system and the resulting system-induced distribution shift endogenous drift. Our central, falsifiable claim is that cross-surface causes are not identifiable in general from marginal per-surface monitoring: different coupled failures can produce the same individual signals while differing in their joint behavior, requiring joint analysis for reliable attribution. This agenda comprises four foundational research problems: fusing heterogeneous drift signals into calibrated gating and attribution (C1); mining agent traces whose activity alphabet evolves (C2); maintaining knowledge fabrics through online reindexing, faithfulness, and compression decisions (C3); and safely promoting concurrent learning streams through one shared evaluation gate (C4). These remain fragmented across concept drift, performative prediction, process mining, streaming retrieval-augmented generation, and MLOps, without a unified data-mining agenda. We outline public benchmarks and evaluation criteria, using AI-native telecom only as a motivating exemplar with analogues in finance, software CI/CD, and clinical decision support."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "5cfacf02e804d95459ca0f7c4188948cfcef329c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.25211", "CorpusId": 293807440}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5cfacf02e804d95459ca0f7c4188948cfcef329c", "title": "NeuroAct: Advancing EEG Analysis and Decoding via Dual-Loop Autonomous Research", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25211, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science", "Engineering"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-18", "authors": [{"authorId": "2360371481", "name": "Wei Xiong"}, {"authorId": "2243033869", "name": "Jiang-Tong Li"}, {"authorId": "2188204871", "name": "Han Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2258307351", "name": "Lin-Xiao Yang"}, {"authorId": "2360353275", "name": "Jie Li"}, {"authorId": "2249726937", "name": "Chang-Jun Jiang"}], "abstract": "EEG analysis and decoding underpin brain-computer interfaces, cognitive assessment, and sleep monitoring. Fixed pipelines and reward-driven search do not explicitly determine how signal structure, representations, predictor behavior, and training dynamics contribute to performance or generalization gaps, leaving intervention choice underdetermined. We introduce NeuroAct, an autonomous research agent framework for EEG analysis and decoding. NeuroAct implements dual-loop autonomous research across EEG foundation models, task-specific predictors, and model combinations. In an inner loop, research, development, and review roles use evidence on signal structure, representations, predictor behavior, and training dynamics to test hypotheses, select analyses or interventions, and refine candidates using performance, stability, and cost feedback. An outer loop distills outcomes into conditional, testable recommendations with task and model conditions, then validates and revises them on subsequent tasks.We evaluate four settings: (A) EEG General Analysis, (B) Cross-task Model Optimization, (C) Single-task Open Research, and (D) Collaborative Prediction. In Setting A, NeuroAct's 80.88 macro-average exceeds BrainAgent's 63.13 by 17.75 points. In Setting B, NeuroAct raises EEGPT's mean balanced accuracy (BA) by 7.34 points and HBN Pearson's (r) from 0.6894 to 0.7362; in Setting C, it improves ADFTD BA by 16.33 points; and in Setting D, it raises Workload BA from 79.69% to 85.02%. Experience reuse raises BCIC-IV-2a BA from 34.66% to 50.81% while reducing LLM tokens by 64.02%, indicating recommendations improve cross-task performance. NeuroAct enables evidence-guided EEG research improved by cross-task experience."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "856719103b0e2b77e483f4ca2a031250453625f5", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.25078", "CorpusId": 293807336}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/856719103b0e2b77e483f4ca2a031250453625f5", "title": "CoEvo-RSI: When Harnesses Evolve, Which Models Should Follow?", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25078, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-18", "authors": [{"authorId": "2354046953", "name": "Jia-Qi Su"}, {"authorId": "2340212538", "name": "Gui-Min Zhang"}, {"authorId": "50878225", "name": "Jiejing Shao"}, {"authorId": "2309546812", "name": "Cong Pang"}, {"authorId": "2410033596", "name": "Tian-Kuo Yao"}, {"authorId": "1807998", "name": "I. Tsang"}, {"authorId": "2327301640", "name": "Hai-Yan Yin"}, {"authorId": "2367752546", "name": "Shui-Cheng Yan"}], "abstract": "Model--harness co-evolution has emerged as a prominent paradigm for recursive self-improving (RSI) agents, jointly evolving their execution scaffolds and intrinsic capabilities. We study the persistent yet often overlooked brain--body mismatch problem in this paradigm, where an evolving harness (body) demands behaviors its model (brain) has not learned or restricts capabilities it already possesses. We introduce CoEvo-RSI, a RSI framework that advances model--harness co-evolution through agent-driven, bidirectional historical inheritance. Drawing on a shared evolutionary archive and execution feedback, a meta-agent coordinates harness development from historical designs with model checkpoint selection or merging and subsequent adaptation under the selected harness, aligning inherited capabilities with evolving execution requirements to reduce brain--body mismatch. Using only supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on its own rejection-sampled trajectories, CoEvo-RSI makes Qwen3.5-9B competitive with larger models across diverse vertical applications, including multi-agent failure attribution, role-aware text-to-SQL, and smart-home instruction following. It surpasses DeepSeek-V4-Pro on TraceElephant (+14.16 agent accuracy) and GLM-5.3 on HomeBench VM (+4.90 success rate), underscoring the need for brain--body alignment and the potential of bidirectional historical inheritance to advance agent co-evolution."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "61ad5be9e012580fdd14b77189a0fc5d4b1473c7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24464", "CorpusId": 293687516}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/61ad5be9e012580fdd14b77189a0fc5d4b1473c7", "title": "Learning to Compose: Recursive Self-Improvement of Shared Executable Stacks for Zero-Shot Long-Horizon Robot Manipulation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24464, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2453994527", "name": "Yang-Zhi Chen"}, {"authorId": "2363891692", "name": "Fei-Ran Liu"}, {"authorId": "2505800464", "name": "Zi-Shuo Wang"}, {"authorId": "2278326150", "name": "Zi-Jian Jin"}, {"authorId": "2158762689", "name": "Xin Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2221288461", "name": "Lecheng Ruan"}, {"authorId": "2510511480", "name": "Wen-Juan Han"}], "abstract": "Individually competent robot skills can fail when composed: tools may lack a required capability, completion signals may misrepresent physical progress, and one skill may leave a state that violates its successor's prerequisites. We introduce Recursia, a recursive self-improvement framework that treats the shared executable stack, rather than individual task programs, as the persistent unit of improvement. The stack integrates tools, skills with execution contracts, handoffs, and verifiers. Source-side execution traces guide failure attribution and the generation of missing tools or repairs to existing shared components. Generality checks, mechanism tests, and paired selection and regression gates determine which changes become the next parent. The resulting stack is frozen and reused through declarative task adapters, allowing source-derived changes to support new compositions without target-side code repair. Experiments cover LIBERO-Pro Long simulation and a physical Franka FR3 equipped with a Franka Hand. In simulation, Recursia improves success over ASPIRE by 5.8 percentage points under Pos perturbations and 15.1 points under Task perturbations. Across three physical compositional target tasks, it improves mean success over the Skill Document Only baseline by 40 percentage points. Analyses of constituent execution, candidate rejection, and inference drivers distinguish local mechanism improvements from complete task success and characterize the cost of feedback-conditioned orchestration. These results examine shared executable interfaces as a concrete mechanism for compositional transfer in robot programming."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "341311ce43a43ec8016e3b0110c04607771b9e00", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24630", "CorpusId": 293686130}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/341311ce43a43ec8016e3b0110c04607771b9e00", "title": "Selm: Self-Evolving Library Management for Multi-Skill Agent Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24630, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2515542932", "name": "Meng-Xi Hu"}, {"authorId": "2236902930", "name": "Yuxuan Hu"}, {"authorId": "2335609031", "name": "Jinbo Su"}, {"authorId": "2446823653", "name": "Ke Wang"}, {"authorId": "2330163563", "name": "Hong-Hao Shi"}, {"authorId": "2506645814", "name": "Wen-Li Wu"}, {"authorId": "2287979930", "name": "Cui-Ping Li"}, {"authorId": "2191043357", "name": "Hong Chen"}, {"authorId": "2404854579", "name": "Jing Zhang"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents increasingly use skills as a lightweight way to acquire task-specific knowledge. Recent work enables skills to evolve from task trajectories. However, these methods often lack systematic management of evolved skills, making it difficult for agents to reuse and accumulate skills across domains, thus limiting performance on subsequent tasks.To reliably accumulate, reuse, and maintain useful skills across tasks, we present SELM (Self-Evolving Library Management), a framework that formulates skill evolution as library-level management. SELM jointly manages skill retrieval, updating, and lifecycle by maintaining skill confidence and utility signals. It introduces utility-aware retrieval to select useful skills, a Map-Reduce framework to generate targeted updates from task-specific experience, and lifecycle controls to retain or remove skills based on their effectiveness. These mechanisms enable the skill library to continuously accumulate useful experience while preserving and pruning skills across tasks, improving agent performance on complex tasks across diverse domains. We evaluate SELM on four agent benchmarks covering diverse tasks and execution environments, and SELM consistently outperforms in most settings."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "80f63c0d884847c4d27502cee2cf79e156556968", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24452", "CorpusId": 293687733}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/80f63c0d884847c4d27502cee2cf79e156556968", "title": "HumanSkill-4D: Training-Free Language Querying of Animatable Gaussian Avatars in Space and Time", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24452, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2274204492", "name": "Junsu Kim"}, {"authorId": "8794013", "name": "N. Kim"}, {"authorId": "2086576", "name": "Dongyoon Han"}, {"authorId": "2274106948", "name": "Seungryul Baek"}], "abstract": "We introduce avatar referring segmentation, which selects regions of animatable Gaussian avatars from language queries. A query can also specify a pose or motion to select matching intervals. This lets users interact with avatars for local editing and motion inspection. However, making these selections with existing language Gaussian splatting methods poses two challenges: adjacent parts and garments are hard to separate and may be referred to indirectly (region ambiguity), while fine-grained motions can look alike in RGB (motion ambiguity). We address these challenges with HumanSkill-4D, a training-free framework that shares a multimodal large language model (MLLM) and geometric operators across avatars. For each avatar, a semantic memory links body-part and garment categories to persistent Gaussian regions and appearance descriptions. The MLLM uses category names and descriptions to select categories by appearance, relation, or function. To find matching intervals, it composes operators into programs that check query conditions on driving joint trajectories stored in a motion memory. These category choices and operator outputs make selections traceable, while composing or adding operators supports new motion conditions. To evaluate this task, we introduce three human-annotated benchmarks and show that HumanSkill-4D outperforms the baselines in part segmentation and 3D and 4D referring segmentation, with compact memories and only minutes of setup per avatar."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0547d476e4b763bb1f106ab0059614d9b854ac16", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24723", "CorpusId": 293805100}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0547d476e4b763bb1f106ab0059614d9b854ac16", "title": "Where Natural Language Ends in Agents: Executable Skill Composition with MetaSkill", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24723, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2510370960", "name": "Meng-Xue Shu"}, {"authorId": "2243894203", "name": "Wei He"}, {"authorId": "2441642890", "name": "Hang Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2449714955", "name": "Run-Ke Liu"}, {"authorId": "2364198997", "name": "Kai Han"}, {"authorId": "2399111239", "name": "Yun-He Wang"}], "abstract": "Agent Skills express open-ended task knowledge in natural language, but composing them requires persistent execution order and upstream-output handoffs. We propose selective formalization at Skill boundaries: coordination relations with stable references and machine-checkable constraints become executable, while substantive task judgments remain within Skills. We introduce MetaSkill, a hybrid representation that preserves complete Skill packages and local instructions while making invocation dependencies and predecessor-text bindings executable through a directed acyclic graph (DAG) runtime. This interface supports explicit coordination without prescribing how each Skill performs its local task. Across 20 multi-Skill artifact tasks and two LLMs, we compare natural-language procedures, prompt-visible graphs, and MetaSkill derived from a common frozen plan per task, using the same designated Skill packages. Additional comparisons evaluate AgentSkillOS with the same available Skill packages as MetaSkill, as well as agent systems using their configured tools. Blinded artifact judgments yield favorable preference point estimates across all reported comparisons, with 95% task-bootstrap intervals excluding parity against Prompt Graph under DeepSeek-V4-Flash and OpenClaw under GLM-5.2. In audited GLM-5.2 execution traces, all planned nodes complete and all declared precedence constraints are satisfied. Together, these findings support MetaSkill as a practical interface for composing open-ended Skills, combining local linguistic flexibility with executable dependencies, explicit predecessor-text bindings, and auditable coordination. Code is available at: https://github.com/TokenRhythm/opensquilla/tree/paper/metaskill-lp4fm-2026"}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6940423e79360933d46ecfb5bd7dd8c2cb57c3b9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22791", "CorpusId": 293453387}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6940423e79360933d46ecfb5bd7dd8c2cb57c3b9", "title": "Self-Evolving Coding Agents via Harness-Policy Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22791, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "122835755", "name": "Zhepei Wei"}, {"authorId": "2363427861", "name": "Wen-Lin Yao"}, {"authorId": "2257158371", "name": "Bao-Lin Peng"}, {"authorId": "2254643264", "name": "Dan-Qing Wang"}, {"authorId": "2339987832", "name": "Qian-Hui Wu"}, {"authorId": "2280195943", "name": "Hao Cheng"}, {"authorId": "145391513", "name": "Yu Meng"}, {"authorId": "2256752511", "name": "Jian-Feng Gao"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents have shown promising potential in coding tasks, and their performance is jointly determined by the policy model and the harness that mediates their interaction with the environment. Indeed, harness and policy are mutually reinforcing and evolving only one component is suboptimal---a harness tuned for one checkpoint may not target the failure modes of an updated policy, while policy learning remains limited to the trajectories elicited by a fixed harness. In light of this, we propose Self-HPO, a recursive self-improving framework for coding agents that alternates harness and policy optimization, using the evolving policy as its own harness proposer. Specifically, in each round, the current policy first analyzes its own failures and proposes new harness candidates (H-step). The policy is then updated through reinforcement learning (RL) under the selected harness (P-step), and hard tasks identified during training form the next harness search set, keeping harness optimization targeted to the policy's evolving failure modes. Across three coding benchmarks, Self-HPO consistently outperforms harness-only and policy-only baselines, improving Qwen3.5-35B-A3B's average resolve rate from 57.8% to 64.8%, with gains generalizing to out-of-distribution settings. Further analysis shows that both optimized components retain gains even when paired with their base counterparts, while their joint gain exceeds the sum of their individual gains. Self-HPO also improves reliability (Pass@1) and expands the capability frontier (Pass@k) over the base agent. Additionally, we show that with a stronger external model (e.g., Opus 4.8) as the harness proposer, the same framework yields a further performance boost."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a3e524fa658b69f591ac9dd7720b51a4127a6063", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22897", "CorpusId": 293452876}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a3e524fa658b69f591ac9dd7720b51a4127a6063", "title": "When to Forget: Risk-Controlled Context Adaptation for Long-Horizon Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22897, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2362980894", "name": "Chen-Leng Chen"}, {"authorId": "2315861110", "name": "Yang-Bo Wei"}, {"authorId": "2514825973", "name": "Zhi-Jie Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2507870689", "name": "Zhen-Yu Su"}, {"authorId": "2260433198", "name": "Qi-Fan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2394493136", "name": "Jun-Hong Qian"}, {"authorId": "2454315569", "name": "Chao Yao"}, {"authorId": "2355015452", "name": "Chen Wu"}, {"authorId": "2265165404", "name": "Lei He"}], "abstract": "Long-horizon agents learn from failure, but the value of a failure record can change as execution progresses. We study when retaining, compressing, or removing the same history block improves subsequent execution relative to full-history KEEP. We introduce RECAP, a framework that combines matched context interventions, execution-relational prediction, and independent policy certification. Matched environment states and rollout seeds measure success and continuation prompt-cost differences; prefix-only relations between a failure block and the current execution propose promising edits. Independent task bundles then select a policy subject to harmful-intervention and non-positive-benefit risk constraints under a fixed measurement protocol. A five-seed longitudinal case holds both the failure block and its summary fixed: compression fails at an early state and becomes a cost-saving intervention at a later state. Across five backbones, RECAP produces positive paired-success saving estimates with backbone-specific reliability-efficiency tradeoffs. On Hunyuan-A13B, it edits 43.24% of retained checkpoints while matching KEEP's observed 91.89% continuation success and reducing unconditional mean continuation prompt cost by 4,619.8 tokens (2.13%). The framework provides an execution-level reference for deciding when to forget: retain history by its value to what comes next."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d8d4aeb074836ea2de4663f8317fb920b8d4b194", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23677", "CorpusId": 293546561}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d8d4aeb074836ea2de4663f8317fb920b8d4b194", "title": "Agentic Physics-Grounded Video Generation via Memory-Augmented In-Context Reasoning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23677, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2376054477", "name": "Xi-Kang Guan"}, {"authorId": "2325641579", "name": "Shao-Cong Yang"}, {"authorId": "2305500652", "name": "Yan-Qi Bao"}, {"authorId": "2510508479", "name": "Can Wang"}, {"authorId": "2302364861", "name": "Zheng Gu"}, {"authorId": "2510015753", "name": "Zheng-Qing Chen"}, {"authorId": "2307929487", "name": "Kai Chen"}, {"authorId": "2055851838", "name": "Jing Huo"}, {"authorId": "2375092142", "name": "Yang Gao"}], "abstract": "Physical plausibility is essential for realistic video generation. Existing methods impose physical guidance before synthesis via simulations or refined prompts, but this can limit dynamic diversity, miss errors that emerge only after generation, and require repeated regeneration when inconsistencies remain. We therefore study a generate-diagnose-repair paradigm that preserves the original generation capability and selectively repairs physical inconsistencies afterward, raising two key questions: what to repair and how to repair? To address them, we introduce PhysRepair, an agentic video generation framework that combines causal reasoning over the observed physical failure with prior repair experience to enable localized and adaptive correction. Specifically, Spatiotemporal Causal Constraints identify the first invalid physical state and its causally affected states, producing a repair specification that guides physically plausible local regeneration while preserving unaffected content. Experience-augmented In-context Learning combines the ongoing repair context with relevant experience from previous repairs, allowing the agent to adapt its repair decisions across diverse physical failures and accumulate completed trajectories for future cases. Experiments on Physics-IQ Verified across solid mechanics, fluid dynamics, optics, thermodynamics, and magnetism demonstrate that PhysRepair consistently improves the physical plausibility of generated videos over existing methods. Our project page is available at https://xxkkxxx.github.io/PhysRepair-Website/."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "5d524f69ae5a50ea3b1d3c975b8e156e8b8b5489", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23186", "CorpusId": 293545952}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5d524f69ae5a50ea3b1d3c975b8e156e8b8b5489", "title": "Code Skill Server: A Large-Scale Search Engine for Code Generation Skills", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23186, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2515007307", "name": "Xiao-Yong Li"}, {"authorId": "2492104523", "name": "Hao-Long Li"}, {"authorId": "2378109439", "name": "Dong-Liang Xu"}, {"authorId": "2512690722", "name": "Hao-Yu Li"}, {"authorId": "2514731732", "name": "Yi-Fan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2515203264", "name": "Jing-Yuan Shi"}, {"authorId": "2473524993", "name": "Yang-Hua Wang"}, {"authorId": "2514731005", "name": "Yan-Bin Sun"}, {"authorId": "2338801282", "name": "Shi-Dong Pan"}, {"authorId": "2378112166", "name": "Yue Yao"}], "abstract": "Agent skills have proven to be an effective technique for improving agent ability on specific tasks, including software engineering, natural science, and mathematics. During such a process, using agent skills often involves manually discovering and organizing skills from heterogeneous sources, while existing skill retrieval methods mainly focus on selecting relevant skills from established skill libraries. However, with the rapidly increasing number and variety of reusable agent skills, manually searching for relevant skills across heterogeneous sources is becoming impractical at scale. In this paper, we introduce the Code Skill Server (CSS), a large-scale search engine for finding the most useful code generation skills for a target task. CSS consists of a skill server that incrementally collects and indexes publicly available code generation skills from multiple sources, with 993 skills included in the corpus used in this work. Extensive experiments across different retrieval methods and code generation benchmarks show that the best CSS configuration improves Pass@1 by up to 13.91% and consistently improves all evaluated models by at least 4.21% in the cross-model evaluation. These results demonstrate that CSS can effectively search for and serve relevant skills, improving code generation quality and enhancing models'code generation capabilities. Code and the CSS-993 corpus are available at {https://github.com/haoyuli-sdu/CodeSkillServer}."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "48c319021a5ca367b6e450f42f2ea659e2cf068a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23098", "CorpusId": 293546335}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/48c319021a5ca367b6e450f42f2ea659e2cf068a", "title": "InstAgent: A Recursively Self-Improving Agent Operating System for Heterogeneous Robot Collaboration", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23098, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2333421879", "name": "Hao-Yang Luo"}, {"authorId": "2514693968", "name": "J. Qin"}, {"authorId": "2507477758", "name": "Jin-Yan Cao"}, {"authorId": "2500923891", "name": "Yi Huang"}, {"authorId": "2514397908", "name": "Guang Yang"}, {"authorId": "2515004138", "name": "Xiao-Yu Chen"}, {"authorId": "2351826252", "name": "Hao-Tao Lu"}, {"authorId": "2363497754", "name": "Tian-Xiang Gui"}, {"authorId": "2192580886", "name": "Ye Shi"}, {"authorId": "2273021500", "name": "Jing-Ya Wang"}], "abstract": "Heterogeneous robot teams enable complex, long-horizon tasks by combining complementary sensing, mobility, and manipulation capabilities across multiple embodiments. Yet existing systems struggle to retain execution-time corrections as reusable experience, maintain task-relevant context memory across space and time for reliable handoffs, and explicitly represent the inter-robot dependencies required for structured collaboration. In order to address these challenges, we present InstAgent, a recursively self-improving (RSI) Agent Operating System for heterogeneous robot collaboration. InstAgent organizes team behaviors as agentic behavior trees (BTs) that explicitly model capability assignment, inter-robot dependencies and handoff checks. The RSI mechanism updates these collaboration programs from execution feedback and preserves successful revisions for reuse in future tasks, while shared multimodal spatio-temporal memory provides context for planning and handoff validation. Experiments on 20 real-world multi-robot tasks with three high-level models show consistent gains in task success, first-attempt completion, and execution efficiency."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "90ed2138c0c1c7c2c5310484c408126d5da21201", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23574", "CorpusId": 293546054}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/90ed2138c0c1c7c2c5310484c408126d5da21201", "title": "Darwin-Claw: Environment-Adaptive Experience Consolidation for Recursively Self-Evolving Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23574, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2297186228", "name": "Ming-Ze Yin"}, {"authorId": "2380043925", "name": "Xiao-Han Wang"}, {"authorId": "2316958318", "name": "Dian Li"}, {"authorId": "2515057402", "name": "Long-Fei Luo"}, {"authorId": "2362798356", "name": "Jia-Hong Yan"}, {"authorId": "2166681156", "name": "Yiheng Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2307736278", "name": "Chang-Yu Hsieh"}], "abstract": "While Large Language Model (LLM) agents have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across complex tasks, they predominantly operate in isolated sessions, lacking the ability to continually learn from historical interactions or adapt to user-specific environments. Existing self-evolution paradigms often indiscriminately inject raw interaction trajectories into memory or skill modules, leading to obscured behavioral patterns and compromised update controllability. Furthermore, as foundational LLM reasoning improves, the bottleneck for agentic evolution shifts from accumulating generic knowledge to effectively managing situational experience. To address this, we propose Darwin-Claw, an environment-adaptive self-evolving framework that systematically distills episodic experiences to drive the comprehensive evolution of operational skills and durable memory. Theoretically grounded in an iterative agent-actor and evolver-critic co-adaptation, Darwin-Claw introduces a Hierarchical Experience Consolidation System that extracts fine-grained error attributions and generalizes them into dynamically updated prototype vectors. This structured situational experience serves as interaction patches for context-sensitive perception and explicit optimization signals for skill refinement. Practically, Darwin-Claw is a readily deployable, automated framework compatible with mainstream agent systems without requiring manual curation. Extensive experiments on WildClawBench demonstrate that Darwin-Claw yields substantial performance improvements, establishing a controllable and stable pathway for the continual, user-centric adaptation of autonomous agents in real-world ecosystems."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7d5e0a51384d0180e2cba203dfd8a55ad8d4b3e3", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23128", "CorpusId": 293546164}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7d5e0a51384d0180e2cba203dfd8a55ad8d4b3e3", "title": "SCOPE: Selective Certification of Program Edits for Embodied Heuristic Learning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23128, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2401060254", "name": "Jun-Yang Shu"}, {"authorId": "2382845752", "name": "Bing-Qing Wei"}, {"authorId": "2267504431", "name": "Jinsong Li"}, {"authorId": "2281226931", "name": "Zhe-Qi He"}, {"authorId": "2380076557", "name": "Yong-Tao Wang"}], "abstract": "Embodied heuristic learning must balance strategic adaptation with the preservation of previously successful behavior. Local repairs may leave structural failures unresolved, whereas broader revisions can introduce regressions that are costly to detect through repeated environment interaction. We introduce SCOPE (Selective Certification Of Program Edits), which uses failed and successful executions as complementary evidence for program revision. Frontier-guided program search annotates a shared diagnostic graph with execution evidence to distinguish supported progress from unresolved checks. Selective behavioral certification uses stateful API replay to restore recorded responses and mutations, checking whether edits preserve selected historical successes. Diagnosis and certification feedback, supplemented by sparse environment diagnostics, guide consecutive repairs across configurations. SCOPE outperforms the strongest baseline in both simulation and real-world tasks. On LIBERO-PRO and Robosuite, it achieves mean success rates of 80.66% and 84.48%, with respective gains of 6.51 and 3.48 percentage points. Our code is available at https://github.com/VDIGPKU/SCOPE."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "00f2980617c653bd7b470cb428980cb4389c41b0", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23288", "CorpusId": 293546354}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/00f2980617c653bd7b470cb428980cb4389c41b0", "title": "CoEvolve: Self-Evolving Code Policies from Skills to Strategies for Humanoid Robots", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23288, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2514746381", "name": "Zi-Cheng Liu"}, {"authorId": "2119798830", "name": "Haochen Wu"}, {"authorId": "2514740104", "name": "Jia-Wei He"}, {"authorId": "2514851796", "name": "Su-Yun Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2454755594", "name": "Huan Yang"}, {"authorId": "2333875987", "name": "Cheng Chi"}, {"authorId": "2514966939", "name": "Hao Tang"}], "abstract": "Recent advances in Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models and World Action Models (WAMs) have expanded the manipulation and whole body capabilities of individual robots. Yet a single robot remains limited by its embodiment, and many real world tasks require capabilities that can only emerge through cooperation. Existing multi robot frameworks largely emphasize task decomposition, allocation, and replanning, while providing limited support for adapting executable coordination when physical execution deviates from the intended behavior. We present CoEvolve, a framework that represents team coordination as executable code over fixed robot motor skills. CoEvolve introduces Verifier Guided Coordination Evolution, where a multimodal foundation model instantiates coordination strategies through motion primitives, semantic objectives, temporary robot roles, and bounded execution parameters. Physical verification evaluates each executed transition, rejected behaviors trigger structured coordination revision from an accepted checkpoint, and verified experience is retained to guide subsequent decisions. We evaluate CoEvolve with two simulated Unitree G1 humanoids transporting a freely supported rigid payload through constrained corridors. Across 15 base task runs with three multimodal backbone families, CoEvolve completes 8 tasks, with every backbone achieving end to end success. Additional evaluations yield verified placement on two additional corridor layouts and after both same-backbone and cross-backbone experience initialization, while exposing persistent limitations in motion precision and contact stability. These results support executable coordination as a practical interface for adapting long horizon multi robot collaboration under shared physical constraints."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8d5aab147839ff408b07f6bd19edd1170c7d6ed2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24090", "CorpusId": 293687223}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8d5aab147839ff408b07f6bd19edd1170c7d6ed2", "title": "Persistent Memory and Iterative Improvement in an LLM Agent for Chinese-Character Drawing", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24090, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2510541248", "name": "Pei-Lin Wu"}, {"authorId": "2265763056", "name": "Yi-Zhuang You"}], "abstract": "Does persistent memory accumulated from previous attempts help an LLM agent solve new problems, and does memory design affect that benefit? We study programmatic Chinese-character drawing across five agent conditions attempting the same 668 items. Accumulated memory produces modest aggregate gains despite substantial growth in the memory stores: free-form notes improve first-attempt success by 4.6 percentage points, while a design combining free-form memory with reusable code improves it by 2.8 points. The two designs do not differ significantly overall, but the code bank shows a suggestive advantage on later, harder items. A separate post-hoc comparison finds a much larger improvement when the code-bank agent receives privileged, target-specific structural supervision. The agent can therefore exploit highly informative structural guidance, although the privileged per-item information does not establish what could have been learned from experience alone. These results suggest that memory design may affect where accumulated experience becomes useful, even when its average benefit is modest."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ba5896dd7b2037f571170607362496bb1aa6e4b0", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24282", "CorpusId": 293686878}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ba5896dd7b2037f571170607362496bb1aa6e4b0", "title": "ROAR: Benchmarking LMM Agents under Real-Time Constraints in Multiplayer Action-Shooter Games", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24282, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2276968555", "name": "Da-Wei Wang"}, {"authorId": "2258097326", "name": "Di Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2515482684", "name": "Jian-Ran Yu"}, {"authorId": "2111167774", "name": "Cheng-Min Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2404975498", "name": "Marci Chi Ma"}, {"authorId": "2473610033", "name": "Xiao-Yang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2323500396", "name": "Bei-Ning Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2154122548", "name": "Shuai Chu"}, {"authorId": "2284689031", "name": "Yun Sing Koh"}, {"authorId": "2557946", "name": "Gary Ushaw"}, {"authorId": "50653199", "name": "Richard Davison"}], "abstract": "Large multimodal models (LMMs) are increasingly deployed as agents that perceive, reason, and act in interactive multi-agent environments. Yet most existing benchmarks for LMM-based agents in multi-agent settings remain turn-based: the environment and other agents pause while an agent performs inference. Such protocols obscure a fundamental challenge of real-time constraints, where the world continues to evolve and delayed decisions may become outdated before execution. Motivated by this limitation, we introduce ROAR, a multiplayer action-shooter game environment for evaluating LMM-based agents in multi-agent settings under real-time constraints. ROAR covers cooperative, competitive, mixed cooperative-competitive, and basic single-agent scenarios. We further propose a Multi-Agent Asynchronous Gym interface that decouples environment evolution from agent reasoning by running the environment and each agent in separate threads, enabling the environment to advance at fixed time intervals without waiting for LMM inference and allowing agents to reason independently without blocking one another. ROAR also features convenient deployment, multimodal observations, and customizable levels. ROAR offers a practical testbed for advancing LMM-based agents beyond static, turn-based evaluation toward real-time multi-agent decision-making."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ee7f54b9eff911bf1efd2dc65eb6758b47bcb76d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23479", "CorpusId": 295588125}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ee7f54b9eff911bf1efd2dc65eb6758b47bcb76d", "title": "HexaShell: A Universal Harness for Robot Coding Agents in Simulation and the Real World", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23479, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2372582333", "name": "Hong-Cheng Gao"}, {"authorId": "2505370090", "name": "Jian-Shu Zeng"}, {"authorId": "2506017930", "name": "Alex Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2502939071", "name": "S. Ge"}, {"authorId": "2502850704", "name": "X. Shang Guan"}, {"authorId": "2105071435", "name": "Ya-Zhe Wang"}, {"authorId": "2506029000", "name": "Ye-Xing Wan"}, {"authorId": "2452617768", "name": "Ze Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2505769176", "name": "Jay Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2514928089", "name": "Xi-Ping Li"}, {"authorId": "2504358350", "name": "Di Wu"}, {"authorId": "2514675625", "name": "Yong-Li Wang"}, {"authorId": "2489547261", "name": "Ling-\u25b2-Yun-\u25bc He"}, {"authorId": "2505389153", "name": "Zhi-Qi Jia"}, {"authorId": "2505392818", "name": "Si-Hang Wu"}, {"authorId": "2502170800", "name": "Xiao He"}], "abstract": "Agents built on vision language models (VLMs) now control robots by writing code, but the strongest of them refine task-specific code over many rounds of trial and revision. Such code tends to overfit the benchmark on which it was tuned, can exploit the benchmark's shortcuts, and is costly to carry over to a new benchmark or robot. We present HexaShell, a thin, task-agnostic harness designed to test how far general tools can go instead. We design a simple and complete set of 14 basic tools, 4 for motion, 5 for perception and 5 for state, which the agent calls in one Python cell per turn. Perception pairs SAM 3, which segments an object from a few words or a click, with NumPy and OpenCV geometry that turns its outlines in several cameras, or a depth image, into its position, size and grasp angle. The API, agent loop and sandbox are shared across all tasks; a new simulator or robot needs only an adapter of 350 to 1,450 lines and a prompt describing its world, and the only task-specific input is an optional skill of about a page of guidance. The same code runs on four simulated benchmarks, built on three simulators with two kinds of arm, and on real robots. With GPT-6-Astra and no task-specific input, HexaShell reaches success rates of 69.9% on RoboDojo, 59.3% on RoboCasa365, 78.3% on LIBERO-PRO and 82.2% on RoboTwin 2.0. With task skills, it reaches 85.0% (score 88.8), 85.6%, 96.7% and 96.8%, respectively, above the best published result we are aware of on each benchmark. The RoboCasa365 result is listed first on its leaderboard. On real dual-arm robots, HexaShell succeeds in 76 of 90 episodes across nine tasks."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3fd4cbb6796cfe85866381ac79b966ef65164ba9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22061", "CorpusId": 293004478}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3fd4cbb6796cfe85866381ac79b966ef65164ba9", "title": "FAOPs: Normal Forms and an Algebra for AI Agent Workflows", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22061, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2034659", "name": "Ali Mohammadi Shanghooshabad"}], "abstract": "Organizations increasingly encode operational procedures as multi-step agent skills, here called Agent Operating Procedures (AOPs), that must be traceable, auditable, reproducible, access-controlled, and bounded in time and cost, as regulated sectors require. Yet AOPs are typically kept as coarse-grained files, in which steps bundle actions, copied procedures drift apart, any step may reach any tool, and refactoring goes unchecked. We present Functional Agent Operating Procedures (FAOP), a formal theory that brings database-grade discipline to AI agent workflows. FAOP recasts each workflow as a typed function over scoped state and subjects an entire library to four progressive normal forms: Decomposed (FNF-1) splits a workflow into atomic steps, Typed (FNF-2) makes its data flow explicit and type-checked, Factored (FNF-3) gives each shared sub-workflow a single canonical definition so a change propagates instead of drifting, and Scoped (FNF-4) checks that each FAOP lists resources used by its own actions and the FAOPs it calls, while guardrails declared by it and by FAOPs that call it remain attached to their sources. A FAOP algebra of provably sound equalities turns refactoring and parallelization into compiler rewrites; orthogonal extensions add optimization (+O), bounded completion with time and cost limits (+B), and reachability checks (+R); and an architectural-level classifier (0-3) determines whether a workflow needs AI at all. Together, these make integrity, isolation, typing, stopping limits, cost, and optimization compile-time concerns across the whole library, not runtime hopes. We implement the theory in SkillBase, an AOP management system (what PostgreSQL is to data, SkillBase is to AOPs), and a separate executor that loads FAOPs from SkillBase and enforces these guarantees at run time. A worked financial workflow illustrates the approach end to end."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "82015d5fc53b587d5258d4d13dd9e3109cc0b56b", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21113", "CorpusId": 292981783}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/82015d5fc53b587d5258d4d13dd9e3109cc0b56b", "title": "SSR: Structural Signal Retriever for Scalable Agent Discovery in LLM-Orchestrated Multi-Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21113, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2389335213", "name": "G. M. Cardenas"}, {"authorId": "3250914", "name": "Carlos A. Astudillo"}, {"authorId": "8394083", "name": "N. D. Da Fonseca"}], "abstract": "Large-scale multi-agent ecosystems require mechanisms for discovering suitable agents without exposing entire registries to a large language model (LLM) orchestrator. The challenge is not only to retrieve relevant Agent Cards from a growing registry, but also to maintain a compact candidate context, since each additional candidate increases prompt size, inference cost, latency, and the risk of exceeding the model context window. We formulate this problem as candidate-context scalability and propose the Structural Signal Retriever (SSR), our deterministic and training-free retrieval method designed for Agent Card discovery. SSR derives structural lexical signals from capability-bearing Agent Card fields, weights them using inverse document frequency (IDF), and uses an Inverted Signal Index (ISI) for selective candidate scoring. We evaluate SSR using a synthetic agent benchmark, a public MS MARCO adaptation, and end-to-end LLM orchestration. At 1,000 agents, SSR reduces LLM input by 98.8% and orchestration latency by 70.9% relative to full-catalog orchestration. On MS MARCO, IDF improves retrieval effectiveness within the structural representation, while the ISI preserves retrieval effectiveness and reduces explicitly scored passages by 96.8%. End-to-end experiments show that candidate pre-selection maintains a compact candidate context as registry size grows and avoids the operational failures observed with full-catalog orchestration. These results support SSR as an Agent-Card-native, traceable retrieval mechanism for candidate-context scalability."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "fb1b5bc9aaad20ca80aaabdfb68347e834dcfb88", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21165", "CorpusId": 292981875}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fb1b5bc9aaad20ca80aaabdfb68347e834dcfb88", "title": "From Long Context to Selective Memory:A Survey of Memory-Centric Compression for Large Language Models", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21165, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "1491637173", "name": "Chao Gou"}], "abstract": "Long-context capability has become essential for large language models (LLMs) to handle complex tasks, yet continuously expanding the context window does not remove the computational, storage, and information-selection pressures incurred at inference time. Existing work on prompt compression, KV-cache management, efficient serving, retrieval augmentation, and agent memory is usually discussed in isolation, leaving the flow of information between storage and computation insufficiently understood. This survey reframes long-context compression as a memory-management problem for allocating inference-time resources. We distinguish compute-active memory, which directly participates in model computation, from compute-inactive memory, which lies outside the computation path but can be stored, retrieved, updated, or forgotten, and we characterize offloaded KV caches, persistent prefix caches, and precomputed KV states as intermediate states on the activation path. On this basis, we formulate writing, offloading, recall, retrieval-and-injection, materialization, eviction, consolidation, and forgetting as state transitions within an information lifecycle, and introduce a budgeted cost model that places these operations in a single cost account. The resulting framework unifies short-term context compression, long-term memory management, and the transitions between them, and shows that the central challenge is not merely reducing information volume but deciding which information should participate in costly inference, when, and in what form. We further summarize evaluation dimensions, benchmark gaps, and open challenges, and argue that future research should move from static, pointwise compression toward dynamic, governable, lifecycle-aware memory management."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c9a41b588537015995c7cf0ba731c58a2ff814b3", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22362", "CorpusId": 293004214}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c9a41b588537015995c7cf0ba731c58a2ff814b3", "title": "CoDA: Co-Evolution of Data Engines and Action Policies", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22362, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2297153733", "name": "Tianjiao Yu"}, {"authorId": "2511794943", "name": "Zhi-Lin Ou"}, {"authorId": "2329388208", "name": "Yi-Fan Shen"}, {"authorId": "2336119887", "name": "Xin-Zhuo Li"}, {"authorId": "2371326711", "name": "Yuan-Zhe Liu"}, {"authorId": "2149412487", "name": "O. Susladkar"}, {"authorId": "2512331284", "name": "Fan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2099420", "name": "Ismini Lourentzou"}], "abstract": "Generative simulation can automate robot environment construction, but physically valid environments are not necessarily useful for training. The challenge is to identify environments that address a policy's failures and supervision that helps it overcome them. We introduce CoDA, a framework in which a simulation data engine and an action policy co-evolve through a shared heterogeneous memory of environment construction, policy failures, and learning progress. The memory compiles validated diagnoses from a vision-language critic into generation programs that recreate difficulties and training programs that help the policy overcome them. We further propose Text2potential to convert these diagnoses into staged potentials for action-chunk value learning, providing dense supervision for policy updates while preserving the task's optimal policy under standard shaping assumptions. Measured learning progress guides further generation and program refinement. Across four manipulation task families, CoDA improves out-of-distribution success from 5.3% after supervised fine-tuning to 27.6%, exceeding the strongest closed-loop baseline by 9.5 percentage points with the same generation budget. On LIBERO-Pro, 15 rounds of generation and training raise success from 25% to 84% under position perturbations and from 1% to 76% under instruction perturbations."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "2b4c6b2ba5c48810d108da02522f37ea9112a89a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21453", "CorpusId": 293003558}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2b4c6b2ba5c48810d108da02522f37ea9112a89a", "title": "CarryOn: A Dual-Loop Harness for Personalized and Reliable Robot Assistance", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21453, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2322451250", "name": "Zixuan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2334922159", "name": "Yu-Qi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2362317400", "name": "Yu-Xin Chen"}, {"authorId": "2276746547", "name": "Sen-Qiao Yang"}, {"authorId": "2515561008", "name": "Jin-Hui Ye"}, {"authorId": "7778612", "name": "Peng-Guang Chen"}, {"authorId": "2251047028", "name": "Chang-Sheng Lu"}, {"authorId": "2278256143", "name": "Shu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2349958453", "name": "Bei Yu"}, {"authorId": "2273012826", "name": "Jia-Ya Jia"}], "abstract": "While generalist vision-language-action (VLA) models have enabled diverse open-world manipulation, real-world robotic assistance requires two critical capabilities they currently lack: personalization and reliable execution. First, executing an instruction like\"make me a sandwich\"requires grounding subtask decomposition in persistent user preferences rather than relying on a single generic solution. Second, reliable deployment demands explainable safety assurance that proactively prevents hazards and gracefully recovers from occasional physical failures. To address these gaps, we present CarryOn, a dual-loop harness designed to augment a frozen VLA policy. The planning loop maintains persistent user-level and scene-level context that is continually grounded against new observations and revised through interaction, enabling personalized task selection and execution grounding across conversations and changing scenes. The execution loop actively supervises policy rollouts, shielding actions from predicted hazardous contact. When execution fails, the system invokes reusable control-based recovery skills that restore task progress and are refined through reflection on earlier failures. On a real robot and in simulation, CarryOn dispatches preference-consistent subtasks across users and scenes, reduces the violation rate of an already safety-finetuned VLA from 52.6% to 6.1%, and increases recovery success from 13% to 80% when refined skills are reused."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "28f53bd88c8bf34d527b0fda596cc7b8ab199749", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21492", "CorpusId": 293003768}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/28f53bd88c8bf34d527b0fda596cc7b8ab199749", "title": "MetaExp: Learning to Self-Improve through Experience via Verifiable Cross-Task Environment Scaling", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21492, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2303429731", "name": "Ren-Hao Li"}, {"authorId": "2471847272", "name": "Xin-Jie Shen"}, {"authorId": "2389123157", "name": "Yang Su"}, {"authorId": "2321581897", "name": "Jianhong Tu"}, {"authorId": "2303402020", "name": "Derek F. Wong"}, {"authorId": "2403252635", "name": "Dayiheng Liu"}, {"authorId": "2346068784", "name": "Min Yang"}], "abstract": "Language-model agents can accumulate interaction feedback, but reliably turning that feedback into reusable experience remains challenging. Existing methods either optimize external experience scaffolds or train experience-related behavior on specific task distributions, leaving open whether experience learning itself can become a transferable model capability. Training such a capability calls for supervision beyond isolated tasks, which mainly support in-episode problem solving rather than revealing what knowledge remains useful across episodes. In this work, we introduce MetaExp, a scalable framework that trains this capability through verifiable task families with controlled cross-task structure. Each family preserves a reusable factor while varying task-specific realizations, enabling transfer without direct solution reuse. We propose two complementary designs: Shared-Tool families keep black-box tools fixed across tasks to elicit operational experience, while Shared-Workflow families preserve latent procedures across changing realizations to elicit procedural experience. We scale environments under both designs, collect and filter sequential interaction trajectories, and fine-tune Qwen3.5-122B-A10B on 4,956 trajectories from 1,000 task families. With parameters frozen during downstream evolution, MetaExp improves by +8.5 points on SpreadsheetBench, peaks at +2.3 on APEX-Agents, and improves by +14.8 on AutomationBench. The gains persist under domain-excluded training, supporting experience learning as a transferable capability for continued non-parametric improvement after deployment."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3194c3d49ef6dbcf5f9a2ae33e2c0df85d6f2f09", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21646", "CorpusId": 293004060}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3194c3d49ef6dbcf5f9a2ae33e2c0df85d6f2f09", "title": "The Self-Improvement Problem", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21646, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2064375154", "name": "Brian B. Moser"}, {"authorId": "50120446", "name": "Stanislav Frolov"}, {"authorId": "2232602491", "name": "T. Nauen"}, {"authorId": "48258541", "name": "Federico Raue"}, {"authorId": "2282138873", "name": "Andreas Dengel"}], "abstract": "With the rise of large language models, agentic systems are becoming increasingly autonomous, as they write code, call tools, revise prompts, and propose modifications to their own components. This autonomy raises a fundamental question for safe self-improvement: can an automated gate like a reward model or an automated reviewer reliably decide whether a proposed update should be accepted? The difficulty is structural. The gate must be a terminating, computable procedure, while the property we ultimately care about, namely whether an update is a genuine semantic improvement, depends on potentially unbounded program behavior. We formalize this mismatch by modeling agents as programs and defining improvement in terms of a semantic score that need not be computable. We study two evaluation abstractions. The first is a certifier that approves or rejects an update. The second is a search procedure that, given access to a proposal generator, attempts to output some improving candidate if one exists. We show that, in the fully general setting, no terminating procedure can simultaneously guarantee reliability (never accepting a non-improvement) and non-conservatism (never missing an available improvement): self-referential constructions can force any such gate or searcher to fail. Randomized gates with bounded error face the same limit. We then identify a standard decidable regime used in practice (bounded evaluation and a finite proposal budget) under which exhaustive simulation yields a terminating search procedure with the desired guarantees. We also show the limit of this recovery: a chain of accepted updates can raise the bounded score in every round while the true score falls. Our results draw a boundary between unbounded, universal settings where correctness guarantees collapse and bounded settings where they can be recovered by design."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0b6fb3c5f486a70c7cc58bc9ef76d0a8f9a2ba58", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22280", "CorpusId": 293004293}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0b6fb3c5f486a70c7cc58bc9ef76d0a8f9a2ba58", "title": "LEASH: Uncertainty-Aware Weak-to-Strong Escalation and Oversight for Computer-Use Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22280, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2309196873", "name": "Qing-Ni Wang"}, {"authorId": "2152046120", "name": "Changdae Oh"}, {"authorId": "2316673843", "name": "Yuzhe Yang"}, {"authorId": "2385968241", "name": "Sharon Li"}, {"authorId": "2325193568", "name": "Jia-Chen Yang"}, {"authorId": "2325491830", "name": "Ang Li"}, {"authorId": "2325785593", "name": "X. Wang"}], "abstract": "Computer-use agents increasingly tackle long-horizon tasks, where running a frontier model at every step is expensive and a small open-weight model alone often fails. Weak-to-strong escalation offers a middle ground where a weak agent acts by default and hands control to a strong one only when needed. The core challenge is deciding when to escalate, which requires an uncertainty signal cheap enough to check at every step. However, even after handoff, the strong agent can still get lost, but a black-box API that the strong agent is usually served exposes no internals to monitor. To this end, we propose LEASH, a training-free framework that unleashes the strong agent only when the weak agent's latent uncertainty signals it is needed, and keeps it on a leash afterward. LEASH has three layers: (1) initial task uncertainty for early routing, (2) step-level uncertainty for runtime escalation, and, after handoff, (3) a shadow observer for continued oversight of the strong black-box agent. Here, we use latent uncertainty as a fuel for all three layers. Specifically, we compare the weak model's hidden states with those from its past successful and failed trajectories, and a state that looks more like the failures signals higher uncertainty. During weak-agent execution, our uncertainty estimator reuses activations already produced during action generation, so it needs no extra forward pass or dedicated monitor training. Experiments on OSWorld across four contemporary model backbones show that LEASH consistently improves over standalone weak agents and outperforms random escalation by 6.55--11.94 points at matched escalation rates. With Qwen3.5-35B-A3B, LEASH achieves 73.66% task success at $0.4117 per task, matching always-on Claude Opus 5 (72.90%) while reducing inference cost by 19.2%."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3c5c84487179b0fd24c07eaac9fffbef4a3227e3", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21427", "CorpusId": 293004557}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3c5c84487179b0fd24c07eaac9fffbef4a3227e3", "title": "Learning from Interaction Experience via Localized Context Distillation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21427, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2347924913", "name": "Zhe-Zheng Hao"}, {"authorId": "2469401065", "name": "Yong-Kang Yang"}, {"authorId": "2307895332", "name": "Xi-Lin Xia"}, {"authorId": "7177756", "name": "Jiarui Yu"}, {"authorId": "2372205286", "name": "Yi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2457015304", "name": "Fan Wei"}, {"authorId": "2265581222", "name": "Pei-Jie Dong"}, {"authorId": "2469116813", "name": "Tian-Fu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2377534360", "name": "Qiang Lin"}, {"authorId": "2279089364", "name": "Can Wang"}, {"authorId": "2371298953", "name": "Han-De Dong"}, {"authorId": "2385486198", "name": "Jia-Wei Chen"}], "abstract": "Learning from interaction experience is essential for enabling large language models to continually improve during deployment. Context distillation offers a promising path: privileged information extracted from experience is provided to a teacher, whose behavior is then distilled into a student model that has no access to it. However, both existing forms of context distillation have limitations. Off-policy context distillation trains the student on teacher-generated trajectories and thus suffers from exposure bias; whereas on-policy context distillation trains on the student's own rollouts, whose states are often uninformative and fail to convey behaviors the student cannot yet produce. To resolve this dilemma, we introduce Localized Context Distillation (LoCD). LoCD first localizes the critical spans of a student trajectory that require revision. The teacher then regenerates only these spans from the student's own prefix, and the student is trained on the regenerated segments. LoCD thereby concentrates learning on the most informative states while keeping exposure bias low. Extensive experiments across math reasoning, code generation, and agent tasks demonstrate that LoCD consistently outperforms strong baselines and achieves more stable training with smaller and sparser parameter updates."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e360bc2d9a1d574d374949eb4cab0dbcdcb18b2e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22289", "CorpusId": 293003900}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e360bc2d9a1d574d374949eb4cab0dbcdcb18b2e", "title": "Improving Environment-Agnostic Agents: The Role of Scaffolding", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22289, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2307358073", "name": "Sergey Rodionov"}], "abstract": "Agents built on stronger language models may increasingly construct useful procedures for themselves, reducing the advantage of developer-supplied scaffolding. We investigate why this advantage persists and whether an environment-agnostic agent can be improved without environment-specific engineering. We evaluate a Codex-based agent in two nearly identical prompt variants on ARC-AGI-3, AGI Maze, and Terminal-Bench. Each variant is deployed with unchanged code, prompts, and general-purpose tools across environments, receiving environment-specific information as input. Changing only an instruction to encourage reporting difficulties and returning control for automatic continuation improves mean scores on both ARC-AGI-3 and AGI Maze, while Terminal-Bench shows small, mixed changes. Yet the general agent remains below an ARC-AGI-3-specific reference, including with GPT-5.6-sol. The reference prescribes organization and control rather than supplying new game rules or solutions. To investigate this gap, we transfer reset decisions to a scripted controller that follows a convention already given to the agent. This small change recovers part of the specialized agent's advantage. A complementary AGI Maze experiment shows that additional tutorial experience can reduce performance. Together, these findings highlight the distinction between having information and tools available and using them effectively. Supplied procedures still matter for the models tested here. The performance gains also come with higher token-based cost. The experiments do not separate better use of information and procedures from additional or differently allocated inference. We provide the EnvTraverse framework and an evaluated baseline agent for testing whether further environment-independent changes improve performance in some environments without degrading it in others."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a8ec8092f9b1e0b3994638076291a9d949c4247f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22319", "CorpusId": 293004109}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a8ec8092f9b1e0b3994638076291a9d949c4247f", "title": "A team of Gemini 4 Argon agents beats Minecraft", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22319, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2210859817", "name": "Maxence Faldor"}, {"authorId": "2108384528", "name": "Satinder Singh"}, {"authorId": "48647153", "name": "R. Raileanu"}], "abstract": "We placed four Gemini 4 Argon agents in a sandboxed Minecraft survival world and gave them one goal: defeat the Ender Dragon. Each agent controlled a player via an open-source library called Mineflayer and coordinated with its teammates through in-game chat and shared memory. No one assigned roles or wrote game logic. The agents planned, wrote, and debugged every program themselves. Researchers could watch the run on a live dashboard and pause or stop it at any time, but they had no cause to intervene. Starting from a randomly generated world on Easy difficulty, the team wrote 2,783 programs and defeated the dragon in 27 hours and 14 minutes, dying 605 times along the way. To our knowledge, this is the first end-to-end autonomous completion of Minecraft by AI agents, achieved using only the standard Mineflayer library without pre-scouted seeds or other game-specific tools written by humans. Winning required the agents to keep a coherent plan across thousands of dependent steps for more than a day. Complex real-world software engineering and enterprise tasks demand the same long-horizon coding and multi-agent coordination. We describe the setup, how the run unfolded, the team's strategies (dividing the work, sharing what they learned, recovering each other's items), and the limits of the result."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "bd55b40184229d1b0a04dc21148b49ba7c258206", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22686", "CorpusId": 293452778}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bd55b40184229d1b0a04dc21148b49ba7c258206", "title": "Multimodal Filesystem Memory For Long-Horizon Embodied Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22686, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2390197675", "name": "C. Su"}, {"authorId": "2507877074", "name": "Jia-Hang Li"}, {"authorId": "2398622512", "name": "Zhen-Ting Qi"}, {"authorId": "2267385326", "name": "Chao-Ran Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2514810516", "name": "Hao-Yu Chen"}, {"authorId": "2387973381", "name": "Sejoon Jun"}, {"authorId": "83546634", "name": "Wei-Rui Ye"}, {"authorId": "2383299859", "name": "Yi-Lun Du"}], "abstract": "Agents operating over long horizons need an effective memory that allows them to reuse their experience throughout long interactions. If the entire history of an interaction is retained, it becomes difficult to find the relevant experience, while compressing it into summaries may result in the loss of visual and spatial details that are required later on. We present \\textbf{MFM}, a multimodal filesystem memory architecture that keeps complementary types of agent memory. \\textbf{Episodic memory} stores the observations, actions, and outcomes, and \\textbf{spatial memory} associates experiences with locations. \\textbf{Semantic and procedural memory} build up general knowledge and reusable methods of acting, and \\textbf{working memory} holds the current plans. MFM makes use of searchable textual records to connect these different representations to the underlying multimodal episodes. The agents are able to find previous experiences by means of contextual cues and to examine the original observations in order to recover details that are missing from their textual descriptions. Through consolidation, the agents convert experience into higher-level knowledge and procedures while still keeping references to the original episodes. In the case of long-horizon Minecraft and computer-use tasks, MFM leads to improved performance with three base models. With GPT-6-Astra, MFM completes \\emph{Mine Diamond from Scratch} and reaches 16 out of 24 milestones on the \\emph{Ender Dragon} task, as against 12.7 for the best baseline. On CUABench-KiCad it attains a mean reward of 0.440, as compared to 0.400 for the best baseline and 0.267 in the case of no persistent memory."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "662e9bd962d801a390a02ebf868bfb15575eb28d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22561", "CorpusId": 293453025}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/662e9bd962d801a390a02ebf868bfb15575eb28d", "title": "FRACTAL: A Self-Similar Multi-Agent Orchestration Architecture with Formal Communication, Convergence, and Depth Bounds", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22561, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "10122218", "name": "Ohad Dan"}, {"authorId": "2514439267", "name": "Abhinav Taliyan"}], "abstract": "We present FRACTAL, a self-similar multi-agent architecture for recursive goal decomposition, in which the same five-component organizational pattern -- Orchestrator, Ensemble, Knowledge Ledger, Decision Maker, Meta-Observer -- recurs at every level of a decomposition tree. We make four formal contributions: (1) a communication bound showing intra-node cost is linear in ensemble size and round count (O(kr)) under a two-phase blind-proposal/stress-test protocol, versus quadratic cost for free-form debate, with an explicit, stated assumption on fixed evaluator count; (2) a two-mode orchestration criterion separating generative and evaluative phases via a measurable diversity-saturation threshold; (3) a supermajority convergence guarantee with explicit probability bounds at stated agreement levels, and a formal depth bound under an operationalized goal-complexity measure; (4) a Bayesian inter-layer escalation mechanism unifying hysteresis and contract-breach signaling under one posterior update. We illustrate the architecture's execution qualitatively on a worked example and a multi-layer case study; we do not report quantitative performance claims in this paper, and we explicitly discuss the formal and inductive-bias limitations of the model, including an unimplemented but architecture-compatible direction toward bounded self-improvement. We use\"fractal\"as an architectural metaphor for self-similarity, not a measured mathematical property. Empirical validation of the architecture's comparative performance is left to future work."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d87329f468319843890f23f1babd4b164c3cd26c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22732", "CorpusId": 293453300}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d87329f468319843890f23f1babd4b164c3cd26c", "title": "TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22732, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2299473032", "name": "Aaron J. Li"}, {"authorId": "2266751372", "name": "Wei-Jia Xu"}, {"authorId": "2261286637", "name": "C. Singh"}, {"authorId": "2404540974", "name": "Zi-Xiao Chen"}, {"authorId": "1827015", "name": "J. Inala"}, {"authorId": "2364686196", "name": "Charlotte Siska"}, {"authorId": "2514443579", "name": "Acar Tamersoy"}, {"authorId": "2346813317", "name": "Bin Yu"}, {"authorId": "2290576964", "name": "Ion Stoica"}, {"authorId": "2253458981", "name": "Michel Galley"}, {"authorId": "2296412141", "name": "Jian-Feng Gao"}], "abstract": "Advances in frontier coding agents have sharply increased the token costs of AI-assisted software engineering. Their token efficiency is typically evaluated on isolated tasks, whereas real-world developers use them to continuously build, update, and maintain large-scale projects. This mismatch motivates us to evaluate agents over sequences of realistic coding tasks, where we find that agents exhibit recurring environment-dependent token costs that existing methods struggle to reduce. To minimize these costs, we introduce TokenEconomizer, an external controller that cuts environment-specific token costs over a sequence of tasks. These cuts come from determining (i) which operational micro-skills to inject, (ii) which persistently irrelevant tools to prune, and (iii) when to intervene in unproductive failure loops. Across Claude Code, Codex, and GitHub Copilot CLI paired with frontier models, TokenEconomizer reduces the average token cost by 18.4-30.9% with comparable task success. Savings hold in every repository under every tested agent-model setting and generalize to diverse domains such as sequential web development and security auditing, where costs fall by 6.4-21.2% depending on task structure. Our work establishes adaptive black-box cost control as a promising direction for reducing token costs of coding agents in realistic settings."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6fb748db583b9ac548f1ed8e8bf55cb13621023e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19843", "CorpusId": 292902321}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6fb748db583b9ac548f1ed8e8bf55cb13621023e", "title": "LAGO: Language Agents with Reusable Growing Options", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19843, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2500250331", "name": "Yu-Ran Wu"}, {"authorId": "2364560731", "name": "Meng-Kang Hu"}, {"authorId": "2400967514", "name": "Q. Qiu"}, {"authorId": "2277883005", "name": "Fan-Qing Meng"}, {"authorId": "2346067745", "name": "Qi-Guang Chen"}, {"authorId": "2295681570", "name": "Yu-Hang Zhou"}, {"authorId": "30024940", "name": "Qiu-Dan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2509715990", "name": "Xu Wang"}], "abstract": "Long-horizon tasks expose a weakness of monolithic tool-use loops: one agent must decompose, execute, and verify a task within a single trajectory. We introduce LAGO, which treats specialized language agents as reusable temporally extended options. A meta-agent retrieves options from a persistent library or spawns new ones, dispatches sub-tasks, verifies results, and admits successful novel options. The library grows across tasks without updating model weights. On 500 SWE-bench Verified tasks, frozen Qwen3.5-35B-A3B in both roles resolves 58.6% under single-attempt evaluation with the official Docker evaluator. On a fixed 150-task subset, we re-run mini-SWE-agent, Terminus 2, and LAGO with the same policy, temperature, task IDs, and evaluator; their resolve rates are 49.3%, 57.3%, and 60.3%, respectively. Cross-model experiments provide evidence consistent with a role for decomposition quality. A 50-task component ablation finds four fewer resolved tasks without decomposition, two fewer with shuffled reused content, and six fewer without the library. These results support reusable agent-options as a practical abstraction for task streams, while leaving component-level causality task- and model-dependent."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b208fc40a451dddc8b427f8c2205f1da6b74156d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.20240", "CorpusId": 292902908}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b208fc40a451dddc8b427f8c2205f1da6b74156d", "title": "PAWA: Panoramic Action-conditioned World Modeling for Multiple Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20240, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2206558642", "name": "Si-Qi Yang"}, {"authorId": "2259866135", "name": "Yimu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2314783462", "name": "Zi-Qi Cai"}, {"authorId": "2205928142", "name": "Cheng-Xuan Zhu"}, {"authorId": "46223195", "name": "Shu-Chen Weng"}, {"authorId": "2351080333", "name": "Erwin Wu"}, {"authorId": "2224390928", "name": "Zhao-Fei Yu"}, {"authorId": "2349384231", "name": "Kai-Peng Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2150089067", "name": "Bo-Xin Shi"}], "abstract": "A multiplayer world model must predict how the independent actions of several players change a shared environment, including changes outside a player's current field of view. A point-of-view (POV) video captures such changes only after they enter its field of view, whereas panoramic video records the surroundings in every viewing direction and has served as an intermediate representation of the world state in recent world models. Learning this task therefore requires synchronized POV and panoramic videos of interacting players together with their actions, yet no existing dataset provides this combination in Minecraft, an established testbed in which players act independently in a shared, modifiable 3D world. We present PAWA, a dataset and framework for action-conditioned panoramic multiplayer world modeling. The dataset pairs each player's POV video with world-stabilized panoramic video and aligned actions. Its distributed pipeline decouples CPU gameplay capture from GPU rendering, scales beyond 64 simultaneous players, and re-renders each recorded session under new observation configurations without replaying the gameplay. On this dataset, we develop a dual-tower model that jointly predicts POV and panoramic video and couples the two streams through cross-attention that aligns tokens observing the same direction in a shared world frame. We further introduce a quadratic-phase player encoding that provides many more player codes than existing rotary codes at the same embedding dimension. Experiments show that PAWA outperforms state-of-the-art multiplayer world models in POV prediction quality while also producing high-fidelity panoramic predictions. The dataset and collection pipeline are open source to support further research on multiplayer world models."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0f11cb71be9ece66cf66aa30348912d50650d2a9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.20560", "CorpusId": 292903117}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0f11cb71be9ece66cf66aa30348912d50650d2a9", "title": "PhySGA: A Physics-Space Grounded Agentic Framework for Generalizable and Interactive 3D Composition", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20560, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2348941463", "name": "Weichen Lyu"}, {"authorId": "2257004568", "name": "Chong Xia"}, {"authorId": "2407234534", "name": "Ming-Yu Dong"}, {"authorId": "2158596679", "name": "Long-Ji Xu"}, {"authorId": "2293868235", "name": "Sheng-Jun Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2295741834", "name": "Yue-Qi Duan"}], "abstract": "3D object composition is pivotal to digital content creation and robot simulation, while also serving as a critical testbed for physical intelligence in foundation models. Previous methods either leverage large language models to directly predict object coordinates, or utilize generative diffusion priors to synthesize composite images for visual alignment, both of which rely on static priors in digital spaces. However, these methods consistently struggle with complex compositions such as precise hanging, gravity-driven settling and hidden containment, as these tasks require interactive construction processes involving global planning, fine-grained alignment, and physical simulation. To address these challenges, we propose PhySGA, a Physics-Space Grounded Agentic Framework for 3D composition via agent exploration and physical simulation, enabling the universal interactive assembly of rigid, articulated, and deformable objects. Directly applying vanilla LLMs or VLMs to this task proves insufficient, as current models inherently lack grounded physical understanding and interactive construction experience. To overcome this limitation, we design a collaborative feedforward-feedback architecture: an Assembly Agent focuses on geometric and kinematic reasoning for global step planning and coarse adjustment, while a Critic Agent reasons over contact dynamics for simulator state diagnosis and fine-grained closed-loop correction. Furthermore, Action Routers map semantic planning and analysis onto executable physical primitives within the simulator, while a task-specific Self-Evolving Skill Library distills historical trial-and-error feedback into reusable experiences, boosting success rates and minimizing exploration attempts. Extensive experiments within Isaac Sim demonstrate that PhySGA significantly surpasses existing methods in relation fidelity, physical plausibility, and visual quality."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "89a674194f28cff61198826066e4fc02a19f698f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19686", "CorpusId": 292902519}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/89a674194f28cff61198826066e4fc02a19f698f", "title": "Interlock: From Local Correctness to Task Completion in Long-Horizon Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19686, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2348311347", "name": "Lin-Ji Hao"}, {"authorId": "2510805115", "name": "Yan-Ji He"}, {"authorId": "2510472394", "name": "Zi-Han Wang"}, {"authorId": "2406893596", "name": "Han-Si Yang"}, {"authorId": "2504338178", "name": "Jia-Yuan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2378579938", "name": "Hong-Yan Hao"}, {"authorId": "2383008230", "name": "Qi Gu"}, {"authorId": "2412097369", "name": "Yan Feng"}, {"authorId": "2453958864", "name": "Hank Wu"}], "abstract": "Large language model agents increasingly undertake long-horizon tasks in complex environments characterized by fragmented evidence, partially completed states, and dependencies across resources and artifacts. Existing agents may satisfy individual requirements while failing to maintain these relationships, producing locally correct but incomplete outcomes. These failures arise because task completion depends on relationships among environmental evidence, consequential decisions, and required effects. To address this completion gap, we propose Interlock, a framework for task synthesis that represents each task as a typed completion graph. Grounding edges connect public evidence to decisions, coupling hyperedges impose joint constraints across components, and realization edges connect decisions to all required downstream effects. Interlock compiles this graph into a resettable workspace, a public request, and an evaluation rubric. A source-coverage review checks whether the original requirements survive generation, while a public-input review checks whether the rubric is supported by the materials available to the solver. An agent-as-judge inspects execution artifacts and runs verification scripts, retaining trajectories judged to satisfy all mandatory requirements. To evaluate training utility, we fine-tune two Qwen3.6 backbones on their own accepted trajectories. Across the two backbones, training improves AutomationBench task pass rates by 4.00-4.50 percentage points, JobBench weighted scores by 3.20-6.64 points, and Harvey LAB criterion pass rates by 3.89-4.16 percentage points. These results demonstrate the training utility of the synthesized experience across external task environments."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "52054481227602b1e6919066a33b3689b3c1d306", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19882", "CorpusId": 292903605}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/52054481227602b1e6919066a33b3689b3c1d306", "title": "BluePixel: Toward Native 4K Agentic Image Editing", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19882, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "51303943", "name": "Shan-Yan Guan"}, {"authorId": "2303281161", "name": "Shi-Yu Li"}, {"authorId": "2379643032", "name": "Xin Li"}, {"authorId": "2350680556", "name": "Zhi-Zhou Chen"}, {"authorId": "2350744574", "name": "Yun-Zhe Xu"}, {"authorId": "30551953", "name": "Yue-Hao Liu"}, {"authorId": "47215940", "name": "Rui-Xue Shen"}, {"authorId": "2479406792", "name": "Shi-Jun Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2465571478", "name": "Min-Da Jin"}, {"authorId": "2510656532", "name": "Qiang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2365969624", "name": "Jun-Bo Liu"}, {"authorId": "2367196849", "name": "Peng Shi"}, {"authorId": "2494263917", "name": "Sheng-Ke Sun"}, {"authorId": "2352220179", "name": "Xiao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2509367410", "name": "Yong Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2539841", "name": "Zhong-Ang Qi"}, {"authorId": "2325153056", "name": "Wei Li"}, {"authorId": "2262963387", "name": "Chao Ma"}, {"authorId": "2325161493", "name": "Ying Tai"}, {"authorId": "2325723271", "name": "Yan-Hao Ge"}], "abstract": "Instruction-driven image editing has reached native 4K, yet how such editors are trained remains undisclosed, and pretraining one from scratch is beyond most teams. The task itself compounds the difficulty: a single end-to-end editor cannot make precise parametric adjustments, match specialized experts, or interpret the vague instructions users actually write. We argue that 4K editing is a system problem rather than a single-model problem, and present BluePixel, which couples a native-4K editing model with an agent harness; the complete training recipe is disclosed in this paper. The model makes 4K training practical: it turns a 1K-pretrained editor into a native-4K one through progressive VAE adaptation that keeps 4K training as affordable as 1K, absorbs a long tail of vertical editing skills through continual learning, and refines the aesthetic-fidelity trade-off with reinforcement learning. Distillation via source-anchored target projection (SATP) then compresses 50 denoising steps to 4, completing a 4K edit in 8.7 s on a single Kunlunxin P800 XPU while preserving task success rate and background consistency. The harness, BluePixel Agent, makes editing controllable: it decomposes user instructions into specialist and general operations, verifies every result with the same reward model that guided training, and retries within bounded attempts. On VINS-4KEval++ (3,305 native-4K cases), BluePixel ranks second among seven models, behind only GPT Image 2 in non-edited-region consistency."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d7ba9ec241f37c28ff5837ded21a8145c235ba78", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.20424", "CorpusId": 292902940}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d7ba9ec241f37c28ff5837ded21a8145c235ba78", "title": "BizAgentBench: Unveiling Hidden Challenges in Deploying Long-Horizon Agents for Real-World Business", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20424, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2441892738", "name": "E. Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2260336914", "name": "Yong-Qi Tong"}, {"authorId": "2510653970", "name": "Xue-Jun Yu"}, {"authorId": "2450638101", "name": "Hang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2381925213", "name": "Pan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2510649875", "name": "Yi-Chao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2510715072", "name": "Di He"}, {"authorId": "2369357651", "name": "Jian-She Li"}, {"authorId": "2446870658", "name": "Jiang-Ming Yang"}, {"authorId": "2290396574", "name": "Wei Wu"}], "abstract": "Existing long-horizon agent benchmarks often emphasize final task success while providing limited assessment of intermediate execution validity. Yet real-world business operations require agents to navigate extensive, potentially outdated or conflicting policies while satisfying critical constraints throughout execution. These requirements are particularly consequential in high-stakes domains such as payments, where intermediate violations can incur financial losses or regulatory consequences regardless of the final outcome. To bridge this evaluation gap, we introduce BizAgentBench, a multi-turn benchmark grounded in real-world procedures from a leading payments company. Its executable environment integrates 789 knowledge documents and 601 discoverable business tools, with consistent policies, states, and tool behavior. The benchmark comprises 50 tasks involving resource constraints, temporal dependencies, prior commitments, and conflicting or outdated guidance. Each task is validated through reference execution, while deterministic checks assess both goal attainment and execution validity without prescribing a single solution path. Experiments reveal substantial limitations even among frontier models: Claude Opus 5 and GPT-6 Sol achieve strict success rates of only 64.0% and 61.3%, respectively. For Qwen3.8-27B, goal attainment reaches 42.0%, but strict success drops to 32.7%, demonstrating how outcome-based evaluation can mask invalid execution. Beyond success rates, DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash averages 191.3 tool calls per run, approximately 70% more than reference executions, revealing substantial execution overhead. Overall, BizAgentBench establishes a testbed for evaluating and advancing long-horizon agents beyond task completion, toward reliable and constraint-compliant execution in complex business environments."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "842066f2cfe3ade2af69303a0f3a03837035b44a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19951", "CorpusId": 292903495}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/842066f2cfe3ade2af69303a0f3a03837035b44a", "title": "AtomSearch: Evidence-Grounded Tree Search for Agent Skill Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19951, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2497901572", "name": "Jiang-Shuai-Chen-Rui-,-Zhang-Rong-,-Wang-Gui-Fang- Tong"}, {"authorId": "2430689890", "name": "Sen Deng"}, {"authorId": "2469306138", "name": "Ming-Pei Peng"}, {"authorId": "2311866886", "name": "Jian-Feng Pan"}], "abstract": "Agent skills adapt frozen models through editable external instructions. Revising several rules at once makes changes difficult to inspect, while judging each local edit only by its immediate score can discard useful intermediate states. We introduce AtomSearch, which keeps revision actions local while using subsequent skill states to guide search. Contrastive atomic editing links each primitive rule edit to failed and successful executions, a behavioral hypothesis, and an intended scope. Skill-State Tree Search (STS) evaluates short edit compositions and selectively retains them as executable states that can be revisited and extended. Outcome memory supplies later proposals with observed repair and regression statistics. We evaluate AtomSearch across four tasks and five target models. With deepseek-v4-flash, it exceeds SkillOpt on three of four tasks, including a 9.68-percentage-point gain on LiveMathematicianBench, and SkillOpt's final skills contain 2.3-19.7 times as many characters. In 67% of completed searches, the best validation state passes through an ancestor that was not highest-scoring at its depth. These results show how local rule edits can support the exploration of useful revision paths and yield compact skills, with additional offline evaluations used to explore alternative states."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e05214760d663efe78256371b09f2dab761b2cde", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19910", "CorpusId": 292903078}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e05214760d663efe78256371b09f2dab761b2cde", "title": "When Non-Adversarial Noise Hurts Learning: Benchmarking Skill Self-Evolution in Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19910, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2393075460", "name": "Tao Yu"}, {"authorId": "2408776350", "name": "Zhong-Tian Luo"}, {"authorId": "2337781398", "name": "Yu-Chen Long"}, {"authorId": "2510650780", "name": "Yi-Heng Lu"}, {"authorId": "2391722304", "name": "Yuxuan Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2498887897", "name": "Yan-Pei Gong"}, {"authorId": "2510746360", "name": "Si-Yi Cao"}, {"authorId": "2374185115", "name": "Yu-Qing Wu"}, {"authorId": "2408472091", "name": "Hao-Peng Jin"}, {"authorId": "2375780531", "name": "Zheng-Hao Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2407027049", "name": "Yu-Jia Yang"}, {"authorId": "2507485315", "name": "Jing-Min Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2450306709", "name": "Yun-Yun Hou"}, {"authorId": "2362877110", "name": "Xin-Ming Wang"}, {"authorId": "2374110360", "name": "Hong-Zhu Yi"}, {"authorId": "2321411959", "name": "Jia-Chen Liu"}, {"authorId": "2510757623", "name": "Si-Rui Wang"}, {"authorId": "2269471632", "name": "Nan Duan"}, {"authorId": "2399836822", "name": "Yan Huang"}, {"authorId": "2185177803", "name": "Liang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2367638723", "name": "Tian-Jin Huang"}, {"authorId": "2505476688", "name": "Yan-Yan Zou"}], "abstract": "Large language model agents increasingly improve by distilling experience into reusable skills, but errors in that experience can persist in learned rules and affect later tasks. Existing evaluations largely examine clean learning or perturbations encountered after skills are frozen, leaving robustness during self-evolution insufficiently understood. We introduce RSE-Bench, to our knowledge the first benchmark for evaluating skill self-evolution under ordinary, non-adversarial noise. It spans four domains and six benchmarks, with controlled perturbations to instructions, environmental evidence, execution records, and outcome--record pairings that preserve the underlying tasks and native scoring rules. The protocol separates noise during learning from input-side noise at evaluation, testing frozen skill stores on held-out tasks. We also introduce \\textsc{RSEClaw}, a method-agnostic framework supporting consistent noise injection and process diagnostics of actual exposure and skill updates. Across three methods and two backbones, noisy learning retains only $25.5\\%$--$42.1\\%$ of the aggregate gain achieved by clean self-evolution, depending on the noise type. Perturbations confined to execution records and outcome--record pairings cause average losses comparable to input-side noise, despite leaving the original executions and evaluations intact. Diagnostics further identify differences in candidate skill text that coexist with small changes in mean held-out performance."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "601729c66535041740dc2bd45d28259af02af196", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.20312", "CorpusId": 292902096}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/601729c66535041740dc2bd45d28259af02af196", "title": "Reflect After Failure: Brain-Inspired Self-Evolving Agents for Embodied Spatial Intelligence", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20312, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2510760228", "name": "Xin Li"}, {"authorId": "2510750484", "name": "Xiao-Huan Gu"}, {"authorId": "2270303036", "name": "Yi-Fan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2382924647", "name": "Shao-Bin Ling"}, {"authorId": "2510639356", "name": "Tian-Li Shi"}, {"authorId": "21735776", "name": "Jin-Gao Xu"}, {"authorId": "2277845056", "name": "Zhi-Meng Yin"}, {"authorId": "2344384666", "name": "Da-Peng Wu"}, {"authorId": "2375396939", "name": "Yun Wang"}], "abstract": "Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have made substantial progress in spatial reasoning, and self-evolving agents further extend these capabilities to embodied environments by learning from interactions. However, current self-evolution remains particularly challenging in embodied spatial tasks, complex and multi-step decisions from partial visual evidence making it difficult to determine why an execution fails. The failure may stem from misperception, insufficient exploration, or incorrect planning, yet execution feedback alone rarely reveals which component is responsible. As a result, simply accumulating interaction experience can reinforce ineffective strategies. To address this, we propose HiMeco, a brain-inspired self-evolving agent for embodied spatial intelligence, inspired by metacognitive monitoring and hippocampal memory consolidation. Through Hippocampal-Metacognitive Reflection, HiMeco diagnoses failures in perception-exploration-planning (PEP), then compares execution attempts to derive reusable strategies and guidance for avoiding recurring errors. It also abstracts interaction programs into parameterized skills, using evidence contracts and execution-based validation to determine their retention and reuse. These strategies and skills guide subsequent interactions, with memory updated only between rounds and model parameters kept frozen. HiMeco achieves state-of-the-art performance on ESI-Bench, and it also improves accuracy on EmbSpatial-Bench by 3.94 percentage points without evolution."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c52aea8a4caad8e61e5bedc6e3f8c70d0afc3613", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.20809", "CorpusId": 292981715}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c52aea8a4caad8e61e5bedc6e3f8c70d0afc3613", "title": "A Self-Improving Agent that Grows its Own DSL: Oracle-Free Operator Induction for Exact Question Answering", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20809, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "1996191947", "name": "Yun-Shiuan Chuang"}, {"authorId": "2412748808", "name": "Yi-Rou Ge"}, {"authorId": "2346438807", "name": "Yi-Xi Li"}, {"authorId": "2511440848", "name": "Yi-Ming Bian"}, {"authorId": "2281612988", "name": "Zi-Jie Pan"}, {"authorId": "2511440173", "name": "Jenna L. Ballard"}, {"authorId": "2401264217", "name": "Uma Kona"}, {"authorId": "2309493012", "name": "Linsey Pang"}, {"authorId": "49036220", "name": "P. Mehrotra"}], "abstract": "A self-improving agent has to decide which new skills to keep, and in practice it keeps whatever earns a high reward, convinces a judge model, or compresses its library, even when the skill is wrong. That is fine until the answers have to be exactly right: over a table of transactions, a total is either right or wrong. We ask a harder question: can an agent grow what it can compute with no reward, no judge, and no answer key, and still be exactly right? A single frozen model runs two nested loops over a small, exact domain-specific language (DSL). The inner loop answers new questions only when two independently written expressions agree on every record. The outer loop discovers its own missing capability and authors an operator for it, admitted only when an independent second implementation agrees and the result obeys a conservation law, all without ground truth. The inner loop roughly triples the answerable-question set, and the outer loop self-extends on disjoint starting sets, inventing operators outside the starting closure; a no-induction control answers none of the new questions. The admission rule is empirically sound: it rejects 138 of 138 buggy operator variants under an independent reference, even one written by a different model family, where the admit-if-it-runs, self-consistency, and compression signals the field relies on admit most of them. Unmodified, the same mechanism self-extends on two further domains with unrelated schemas, business-ledger and clinical-encounter question answering, growing their answerable sets more than tenfold and nearly twentyfold. The loop transfers across model families too: run with further proposers, the gate stays sound and independent families self-extend comparably, with only the induction yield tracking each proposer's DSL fluency."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1575f50952e54a61010c49d10e25b25d563189b4", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21016", "CorpusId": 292982320}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1575f50952e54a61010c49d10e25b25d563189b4", "title": "CausalEvo: Causal Skill Self-Evolution for Health LLM Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21016, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2509341066", "name": "Yu-Chen Zhang"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents for health can accumulate reusable textual skills from task feedback, such as calculation criteria, measurement time points, and formula units. Deciding which skills to retain is consequential: useful rules improve later answers, while faulty generalizations propagate errors. We propose CausalEvo, a method for causal skill self-evolution that estimates the relative effects of deliberately changed skill sets while holding the model weights, feedback cases, and rule count fixed. At each evolution step, a frozen LLM proposes candidate rules, which are pooled with the incumbent skills so that the current skill set remains a feasible choice. Paired skill replacements against shared background rules are scored on the same feedback cases; least squares converts the score differences into relative skill values, and the top-ranked rules are retained, favoring incumbents on ties. A decision-loss bound separates candidate support, approximation, and estimation errors. On six MedCalc-Bench-Verified tasks with 120 held-out cases, CausalEvo achieves 60.00% accuracy, compared with 55.83% for SkillOpt and 51.67% without skills. Across the recorded training configurations, which use different optimization budgets, it consumes 55.6% fewer training tokens than SkillOpt. Gains concentrate in particular calculations alongside regressions on others, and paired accuracy intervals include zero. CausalEvo turns task feedback into controlled decisions about skill retention and replacement. Project repository: https://github.com/txbrook/CausalEvo"}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "eecda8b2d1259f1602e6129a4833d007528616a9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.20926", "CorpusId": 292981835}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/eecda8b2d1259f1602e6129a4833d007528616a9", "title": "SkillNEO: Non-Elitist Evolutionary Optimization of Agent Skills", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20926, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2387928717", "name": "En-Ze Ma"}, {"authorId": "2344761288", "name": "Chun-Yu Miao"}, {"authorId": "2299292789", "name": "Yan-Kai Chen"}, {"authorId": "2364546424", "name": "Jie Yang"}, {"authorId": "2298840870", "name": "Lian-Cheng Fang"}, {"authorId": "2307008095", "name": "Ke-Ning Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2476469439", "name": "Han-Rong Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2512006244", "name": "Mu-Yao Xu"}, {"authorId": "2392624228", "name": "Xin-Le Yu"}, {"authorId": "2404547651", "name": "Cheng-Ze Li"}, {"authorId": "2359100511", "name": "Wei-Chieh Huang"}, {"authorId": "2511848449", "name": "Zi-Wei Ren"}, {"authorId": "2243358440", "name": "Yun-Hao Du"}, {"authorId": "2268394001", "name": "Zhi-Xiang Shen"}, {"authorId": "2405783435", "name": "Xue Liu"}, {"authorId": "2375326707", "name": "Philip S. Yu"}, {"authorId": "2408873368", "name": "Zhen Wang"}], "abstract": "Agent skills turn procedural knowledge into reusable artifacts, allowing LLM agents to accumulate and refine expertise through experience. Building on this, skill evolution iteratively improves skills using execution feedback. However, improvement may require several interdependent edits, so low-reward versions can still be worth developing. Requiring immediate reward gains can discard useful partial repairs before their benefits emerge. Non-elitist evolution allows new versions to receive further edits despite scoring below earlier ones. For agent skills, this calls for evidence of what to preserve and repair next. We introduce SkillNEO (Non-Elitist Evolutionary Optimization for Agent Skills), which separately selects a version to use and versions to keep improving. Comparing how related versions perform the same task reveals working steps to preserve and remaining failures to fix. It can also revisit and revise earlier versions using later evidence. Across 86 SkillsBench tasks in eight domains, including software engineering and office work, SkillNEO raises GPT-5.4 Mini's average reward from 49.1% to 66.4%, within GPT-5.5 and GPT-5.6 Luna's original-skill range. Mini executes tasks, proposes revisions, and edits skills; final evaluation uses the same task instances. Gains span all eight domains. The same skills improve stronger models without further editing. On the 41 tasks with modified skills, reuse improves Luna by 10.5 percentage points and places GPT-5.5 10.6 points above its published original-skill reference. Execution records show these skills can benefit stronger models even when Mini still fails some task requirements. Search histories also show lower-scoring versions leading to skills ultimately selected for use. Together, these findings show that skill evolution can turn a weaker model's partial progress into reusable procedural knowledge for improving more capable agents."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8f83cef82e2c2e8a007ea34e9573dcde30a3209a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.20665", "CorpusId": 292982851}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8f83cef82e2c2e8a007ea34e9573dcde30a3209a", "title": "The World Won't Wait: Evaluating and Teaching Agents Under a Live Clock", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20665, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2474919145", "name": "Ning-Xin Pan"}, {"authorId": "2447750407", "name": "Han-Yu Li"}, {"authorId": "2352851777", "name": "Ye-Qing Shen"}, {"authorId": "2511445614", "name": "Ning Ding"}, {"authorId": "2408587961", "name": "Ye-Hui Tang"}, {"authorId": "2108232070", "name": "Wen-Tao Zhang"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents can solve complex interactive tasks, yet reasoning and tool calls take time. In continuously changing environments, an otherwise correct action may arrive too late. We introduce RealtimeBench, with 180 tasks across six game types, to evaluate agents while games continue running during inference. Paired evaluations show that GPT-6.1-sol outperforms the faster Jev when games are paused, but falls behind when they keep running. We propose Two-Timescale Offline Recursive Self-Improvement (TORSI): a slow teacher studies failures and tests corrections in paused games between attempts, then writes reusable instructions for a fast student. The student acts without teacher calls during gameplay, and validation scores determine which updates are adopted. Two rounds raise Jev's average test score from 13.51 to 24.19 on a 0--100 progress scale, improving all six game types. The instructions also improve average scores for three other models without retuning. These findings point toward real-time intelligence: enabling powerful LLM agents to remain effective in a continuously changing world by organizing deliberation, learning, and action across timescales."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b16b80a648553146a8be8f184ef112a3cb0e17a6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23726", "CorpusId": 293546508}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b16b80a648553146a8be8f184ef112a3cb0e17a6", "title": "RedDarwin: Evolving Jailbreak Skills against Frontier LLMs", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23726, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2458321075", "name": "Zhou-Yuan Ma"}, {"authorId": "2374911264", "name": "Yu-Tao Wu"}, {"authorId": "1753845931", "name": "Hanxun Huang"}, {"authorId": "2328107098", "name": "Xiang Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2340824747", "name": "Yi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2387879011", "name": "Xing-Jun Ma"}], "abstract": "Red teaming must continually adapt as frontier LLM safeguards evolve, yet existing approaches largely search for adversarial prompts or optimize within fixed attack procedures. We observe that even when an attack becomes ineffective, its underlying mechanisms can remain useful and can be revised or recombined to construct stronger attacks. Based on this insight, we introduce RedDarwin, an agentic framework that shifts the unit of jailbreak evolution from prompts and fixed attacks to executable attack skills. RedDarwin consists of 47 reusable base skills and autonomously revises, composes, executes, and selects them using target-model feedback, allowing successful discoveries to become building blocks for subsequent evolution. RedDarwin produces 65 evolved skills, improving 44 of 47 base attacks and increasing their mean attack success rate (ASR) on AdvBench from 18.8% to 47.3%. These evolved skills consistently outperform baselines on the held-out StrongREJECT and frontier LLMs unseen during evolution; with only 10 target-model queries, RedDarwin achieves 100% ASR on GPT-5.6-Sol, 92% on Claude-Sonnet-5, and 80% on Gemini-3.8-Flash. Analysis of the evolution trajectories further shows that strong attacks often emerge from novel combinations of reusable mechanisms rather than from individually dominant ones. We believe this paradigm can accelerate adaptive red teaming and inform stronger safeguards, creating complementary value for both safety evaluation and defense."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8a9eb3f91cb1fb69854e840b2c7fd89a31e85d8b", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.1007/s44163-026-02468-6", "CorpusId": 292843947}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8a9eb3f91cb1fb69854e840b2c7fd89a31e85d8b", "title": "Large language model multiagent systems for industrial problem discovery and prioritization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s44163-026-02468-6.pdf", "status": "GOLD", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1007/s44163-026-02468-6?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1007/s44163-026-02468-6, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "101894536", "name": "M. Adoul"}, {"authorId": "2161368912", "name": "Abdelhamid Ghoul"}], "abstract": "A persistent gap separates academic research priorities from the operational challenges faced by manufacturing firms. Established identification methods, among them Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA) workshops, Lean audits and consultant engagements, are episodic, bounded by participant expertise and organised along departmental lines, which makes cross-departmental issues hard to surface. Multi-agent systems built on large language models (LLMs) have meanwhile shown collaborative problem-solving ability in software engineering and, more recently, in shopfloor scheduling and supply chain coordination. This article is a Perspective. It develops a framework and a research agenda; implementation and empirical evaluation fall outside its scope and are set out as its first research priorities. Three contributions are offered. The first is a synthesis of literature from industrial problem identification, LLM-based autonomous agents and manufacturing AI, which positions the proposal against existing industrial LLM agent work. The second is a framework in which six domain-specialised agents (production, quality, maintenance, supply chain, safety, energy) would collaborate through a five-phase protocol: worker-driven discovery, cross-departmental analysis, candidate-relationship detection, consensus-based financial assessment and debate-driven prioritisation. The prioritisation procedure is fully specified, including the normalisation of financial estimates, a measure of estimate dispersion, the derivation of criteria weights, a non-compensatory gate for safety-critical items and a mandatory human verification step. The third is a worked calculation on a hypothetical steel manufacturing vignette, included so that the procedure can be reproduced and scrutinised rather than as evidence of effectiveness. A preliminary qualitative readiness assessment and a three-horizon agenda of fourteen research questions complete the paper. The benefits identified are presented as hypotheses for that agenda to test rather than as demonstrated outcomes."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "dcc2fbfbc8fdb2f8c8b0b29d6bb08bbc4e3933c7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.18813", "CorpusId": 292894673}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/dcc2fbfbc8fdb2f8c8b0b29d6bb08bbc4e3933c7", "title": "Self-Evolving Agent Harness", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18813, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2174671074", "name": "Chu-Yang Wei"}, {"authorId": "2333357871", "name": "Hao-Xiang Guan"}, {"authorId": "2203918573", "name": "Ming-Hang Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2434954462", "name": "Ken Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2367749242", "name": "Ji-Yan He"}, {"authorId": "46223995", "name": "Xi-Ren Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2468000469", "name": "Huan-Huan Chen"}, {"authorId": "2461908277", "name": "Yi-Fei Shen"}], "abstract": "Harness self-evolution revises an agent's software scaffolding, including prompts, memory, workflows, and executable components, while keeping model weights fixed. We formalize a feedback-driven process in which accepted revisions persist across runs. The framework separates update targets from proposal and admission mechanisms. It distinguishes changes to interpreted state from revisions to tools and code, repository-level control logic, and plugin composition. We characterize search through evolutionary optimization and examine its reliability through continual PAC-learning. Under explicit assumptions, the analysis yields an expected generalization bound, a retention bound conditional on historical regression budgets, and a high-probability admission rule using fresh, independent validation data. We examine how persistent harness revisions affect knowledge sharing and organizational workflows, with implications for scientific reliability, physical safety, and institutional accountability. Cases from prior work illustrate model--harness co-design, distributed execution, and plugin reuse. Reliable deployment requires continual validation as task distributions and evaluators change, with independent safeguards for learning, runtime privileges, and consequential actions. We provide a comprehensive 66-page discussion of related work, theory, algorithms, evaluation, applications, and the broader impact of Harness RSI, together with continuously updated code and supplementary materials, at https://github.com/zgcagi/Harness-RSI."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c8a007b733acdd39b4d599a29c5aa436cc9223f0", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.17804", "CorpusId": 292894087}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c8a007b733acdd39b4d599a29c5aa436cc9223f0", "title": "Learning from fracture: Teaching language models to design through physical experience", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17804, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Physics", "Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2258719546", "name": "Bo Ni"}], "abstract": "How can language models learn to optimize physical designs through experience? We introduce FracLearn, an experience-abstraction-generalization framework evaluated through a deliberately unfamiliar, Tetris-inspired fracture game. The model first proposes architectures and examines simulated fracture responses to accumulate experience. Abstraction organizes these observations into conditional principles linking reinforcement placement, crack evolution and load transfer, with supporting evidence and failure conditions. Generalization challenges the model to combine these principles in new architectures and surpass the earlier design record. Campaigns demonstrate progressive refinement and recovery through hypothesis revision, yielding a best composite score 11.0% above the experience-stage record. Fracture analyses connect improved performance to crack deflection and temporary trapping as toughening mechanisms, alongside greater residual load capacity. Learning occurs through external knowledge and decisions, without model-weight updates. This approach offers a route to scientific agents that turn computational experiments into reusable physical knowledge for materials discovery and engineering design."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "565fb1cf42ab53e862ddddbeed66ee2afa450a29", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.18866", "CorpusId": 292895477}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/565fb1cf42ab53e862ddddbeed66ee2afa450a29", "title": "AutoAgent0: An Agentic Runtime for Safe Closed-Loop Driving", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18866, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2298930379", "name": "Jia-Geng Liu"}, {"authorId": "2316585215", "name": "Seth Z. Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2292466757", "name": "Ze-Wei Zhou"}, {"authorId": "153652199", "name": "A. Huerta"}, {"authorId": "2510523474", "name": "Aidan Bayer-Calvert"}, {"authorId": "2377752532", "name": "Yu Bao"}, {"authorId": "2294514916", "name": "Hao-Tian Lin"}, {"authorId": "2510524546", "name": "Robert Spataru"}, {"authorId": "2364703176", "name": "Zi-Jun Cui"}, {"authorId": "2328344870", "name": "Yi-Ning Hong"}, {"authorId": "2316674209", "name": "Bo-Lei Zhou"}, {"authorId": "144303757", "name": "Jia-Qi Ma"}], "abstract": "Safe autonomous driving requires more than accurate planning at a single frame: it demands reliable closed-loop execution in dynamic environments. Although end-to-end driving policies have advanced rapidly, the accumulated closed-loop errors, especially in unseen scenarios, can push vehicles into states from which the same policy struggles to recover. We address the challenge by treating closed-loop driving safety as a test-time execution problem. We introduce AutoAgent0, an agentic runtime and harness framework that augments existing driving experts with complementary detection skills, rule-based action verification, and feedback-driven recovery. To address inaccurate scene perception, an independent detection module constructs scene context using complementary perception skills, and a rule-based verifier checks proposed trajectories against this context before execution. When the nominal trajectories are unreliable, the orchestrator of the runtime activates Recovery Planning, where an agentic loop constructs alternative action proposals from action primitives and iteratively refines them using verification feedback. This design combines independent scene assessment with feasibility checks and online recovery, addressing both perception errors and the limited recovery capabilities of individual experts. Evaluations on Fail2Drive and NavSafe-$\\infty$ demonstrate improved closed-loop robustness over single-expert execution in long-tail and safety-critical scenarios. These results demonstrate that runtime supervision can improve closed-loop robustness across multiple driving experts without retraining the underlying policies."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a4b43eb1827cf6d99800cdf42902eb74c61da74e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.18792", "CorpusId": 292895945}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a4b43eb1827cf6d99800cdf42902eb74c61da74e", "title": "SEAR: Self-Evolving Agentic Robotics", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18792, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2509898711", "name": "Xin-Yi Wang"}, {"authorId": "2261387782", "name": "Bang-Zheng Li"}, {"authorId": "2506680028", "name": "Han-Yu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2504090520", "name": "Lin Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2196998835", "name": "Andrea Yaoyun Cui"}, {"authorId": "2510651346", "name": "Zheng-Gang Tang"}, {"authorId": "8983218", "name": "S. Buch"}, {"authorId": "2510659666", "name": "De-Jia Xu"}], "abstract": "Frontier language-model agents can now improve robots on their own: they write control code, test it, and keep what works as skills and memories for the next task. We introduce SEAR (Self-Evolving Agentic Robotics), a framework and benchmark built around two questions. Is the agent really learning, or just trying more often? And does what it learns still help on new tasks, in a new simulator, and on a real robot? The framework runs an agent in three modes: it writes code that becomes the policy (code-as-policy), acts live in the control loop (agent-as-policy), or works as a researcher that fine-tunes a vision-language-action model (auto-research). The benchmark adds controls that separate retained experience from repeated attempts, familiar tasks, privileged simulator state, larger budgets, and luck. Across seven frontier language models, 330 simulated and five physical tasks, and over four years of agent runtime, we highlight three findings. First, the learning curve of a robotic agent depends both on the overall time budget and the task difficulty relative to model capability. Harder tasks make longer sessions worthwhile; on easier tasks, re-rolling short attempts does just as well. Second, control code and shared routines transfer across tasks and simulators, while task-specific solutions do not. On a real robot, we observe a performance gap between agent-designed practice and novel human-designed scenes. Third, different models evolve more effectively in different ways. Some do best acting live, others writing code first. And a text-only model keeps pace with a comparable multimodal one: perception helps with error assessment, but agentic reasoning and tool use can matter more. We release the benchmark and codebase to support future research."}}]}
